hadiths[448]{hadithnumber,text}:
"16743","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ posed a question to his Companions about the finest and purest deed in the sight of their Sovereign, surpassing charity and battle in rank, and then named it. > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of your deeds, the purest with your Sovereign, the highest in raising your ranks, better for you than giving gold and silver, and better for you than meeting your enemy so they strike your necks and you strike theirs? It is the remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic.""**"
"16744","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports two closely related statements of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exalting remembrance above all deeds in salvation and rank, the second framed as a question to which the Companions assented. > **""No deed of the son of Adam is more saving for him from the punishment of Allah than the remembrance of Allah; shall I not inform you of the best of your deeds for you, the purest with your Sovereign, the highest in your ranks, better for you than giving gold and silver, and better than meeting your enemy tomorrow so you strike their necks and they strike yours? Yes, O Messenger of Allah. It is the remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic.""**"
"16745","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared remembrance the most saving deed, and when questioned about jihād, made a single exception, repeating his answer three times. > **""No deed of the son of Adam is more saving for him from the punishment of Allah, exalted be He, than the remembrance of Allah, exalted be He. Not even jihād? Not even jihād, unless one strike with his sword until it breaks.""**"
"16746","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the single source of regret for the people of Paradise concerning their worldly hours. > **""The people of Paradise will feel regret for nothing except an hour that passed by them in which they did not remember Allah, exalted be He, therein.""**"
"16747","Mālik ibn Yukhāmir reports that Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, related the final counsel he received upon parting from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after asking which deed Allah loves most. > **""That you die while your tongue is moist with the remembrance of Allah.""**"
"16748","Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a dialogue in which a questioner asked successively about jihād, the righteous, prayer, zakāh, ḥajj and charity, receiving the same criterion each time, until Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, remarked to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, on the triumph of the rememberers, which the Prophet ﷺ confirmed. > **""Which jihād brings greatest reward? Those most abundant in remembrance of Allah, blessed and exalted be He. Which righteous folk gain greatest reward? Those most abundant in remembrance of Allah. O Abū Ḥafṣ, the rememberers have taken all good. Yes, indeed.""**"
"16749","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, raises to the Prophet ﷺ a statement making remembrance the most saving deed from punishment, with a question about jihād and a single martial exception. > **""No deed of the son of Adam is more saving for him from punishment than the remembrance of Allah, exalted be He. Not even jihād in the path of Allah? Not even jihād in the path of Allah, except that one strike with his sword until it breaks.""**"
"16750","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prescribed abundant remembrance for whoever lacks strength for night worship, generosity with wealth, or courage against the enemy. > **""Whoever among you is unable to endure the night, is miserly with wealth to spend it, and cowardly toward the enemy to fight him, let him increase in the remembrance of Allah.""**"
"16751","Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ compared a distributor of dirhams with one engaged in remembrance, judging the latter superior. > **""If a man had dirhams in his lap distributing them, and another remembered Allah, the remembrance of Allah would be superior.""**"
"16752","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed remembrance above every charity. > **""No charity is superior to the remembrance of Allah.""**"
"16753","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when he sought counsel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined God-consciousness, remembrance in every place, and matching secret and open sins with fresh repentance. > **""Hold to the fear of Allah as much as you are able; remember Allah beside every stone and tree; and whatever evil you do, renew repentance to Allah for it, the secret with the secret and the open with the open.""**"
"16754","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports in an athar that his intimate friend ﷺ counselled him to abundant remembrance of Allah."
"16755","Umm Anas, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that when she sought counsel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined abandoning sins as the best emigration, guarding obligations as the best jihād, and abundant remembrance as the most beloved offering to Allah. > **""Abandon sins, for it is the best emigration; guard the obligations, for it is the best jihād; and increase in the remembrance of Allah, for you will bring nothing to Allah more beloved to Him than abundant remembrance of Him.""**"
"16756","Umm Anas, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a righteous deed while expressing hope to accompany him in the highest Paradise, and he directed her to prayer, abandonment of sins, and abundant remembrance. > **""Establish prayer, for it is the greatest jihād; abandon sins, for it is the best emigration; and remember Allah abundantly, for it is the most beloved deed to Allah that you meet Him with.""**"
"16757","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, declares in an athar his preference for spending a full day until night in remembrance over furnishing fine horses for the same span."
"16758","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that during a journey the Messenger of Allah ﷺ twice asked after the forerunners, and when the people spoke of those ahead and behind, he identified the true forerunners and the path to the meadows of Paradise. > **""Where are the forerunners? Where are the forerunners, those absorbed in the remembrance of Allah? Whoever wishes to graze in the meadows of Paradise, let him increase in the remembrance of Allah.""**"
"16759","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that on a journey the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that the mufarridūn had outstripped, and upon inquiry explained their devotion and its lightening effect unto the Resurrection. > **""The mufarridūn have outstripped. Who are the mufarridūn, O Messenger of Allah? Those set apart by the remembrance of Allah; remembrance has laid their burdens from them, so they will come on the Day of Resurrection light.""**"
"16760","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that the mufarridūn had outstripped, and upon inquiry defined them by absorption in remembrance. > **""The mufarridūn have outstripped. Who are the mufarridūn, O Messenger of Allah? Those absorbed in the remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic.""**"
"16761","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded such abundant remembrance that onlookers would deem the rememberer mad. > **""Increase in the remembrance of Allah until they say: mad.""**"
"16762","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded such remembrance that the hypocrites would accuse the rememberers of ostentation. > **""Remember Allah with remembrance until the hypocrites say: you are showing off.""**"
"16763","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah will announce on the Day of Resurrection that the assembled will learn who the noble are, and upon inquiry identified them. > **""On the Day of Resurrection the assembled will know who the people of nobility are. Who are the people of nobility, O Messenger of Allah? The gatherings of remembrance in the mosques.""**"
"16764","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the heavenly announcement granted to sincere remembrance gatherings seeking only Allah's Face. > **""No people gather remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, seeking only His Face thereby, except a caller from heaven calls to them: rise forgiven, for your evil deeds have been replaced with good deeds.""**"
"16765","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him, habitually invited Companions to an hour of faith, and when an angered man complained that he diverted from the Prophet's faith to an hour's faith, the Prophet ﷺ answered with mercy and praise of such gatherings. > **""May Allah have mercy on Ibn Rawāḥah; he loves the gatherings over which the angels boast.""**"
"16766","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him, reminding his companions, declared them the company he was commanded to keep, recited the verse of patience with morning and evening supplicants, and described angels joining, ascending, reporting, and hearing divine pardon even for the erring sitter. > **""You are indeed the company whom Allah commanded me to keep myself patient with; no number of them sits except an equal number of angels sits with them; if they glorify Allah the angels glorify, if they praise Allah the angels praise, if they magnify Allah the angels magnify; then they ascend to the Lord, Who knows better, and say: our Lord, Your servants glorified You so we glorified, magnified You so we magnified, praised You so we praised; and our Lord says: My angels, I call you to witness that I have forgiven them; though they say: among them is erring so-and-so, He says: they are a people whose companion is never wretched.""**"
"16767","Suhayl ibn Ḥanẓalah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness and exchange of deeds to those who sit in remembrance and then rise. > **""No people sit in a gathering remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, therein and then rise except it is said to them: rise, for Allah has forgiven you and your evil deeds have been replaced with good deeds.""**"
"16768","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people concerning angelic detachments at earthly remembrance gatherings, the identity of Paradise's meadows, and the measure of one's standing with Allah. > **""O people, Allah has detachments of angels who revere Allah and halt at the gatherings of remembrance on earth, so graze in the gardens of Paradise. Where are the gardens of Paradise, O Messenger of Allah? The gatherings of remembrance; go morning and evening in the remembrance of Allah and remember Him inwardly; whoever wishes to know his standing with Allah, let him look at Allah's standing with him, for Allah places the servant where he places Him from himself.""**"
"16769","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ the account of travelling angels enveloping remembrance circles, reporting their glorification, recitation, blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and worldly and otherworldly supplication, and receiving a repeated command of mercy even for the brief erring visitor. > **""Our Lord, we came upon servants of Yours magnifying Your blessings, reciting Your Book, sending blessings upon Your Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, and asking You for their Hereafter and world. Cover them with My mercy. O our Lord, among them is erring so-and-so who only embraced them briefly. Cover them with My mercy, for they are companions whose companion is never wretched.""**"
"16770","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold luminous peoples envied though neither prophets nor martyrs, and when a Bedouin knelt seeking their description, identified them by mutual love and remembrance. > **""Allah will raise peoples on the Day of Resurrection with light in their faces, upon pulpits of pearl, envied by people though neither prophets nor martyrs. Describe them for us. They are those who love one another for Allah, from diverse tribes and lands, gathering upon the remembrance of Allah and remembering Him.""**"
"16771","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe honoured men at the right of the Most Merciful, envied for seat and nearness, and upon inquiry traced their rank to remembrance gatherings and choice speech. > **""At the right of the Most Merciful, both of Whose Hands are right, will be men neither prophets nor martyrs, the whiteness of their faces overwhelming the beholder, envied by the prophets and martyrs for their seat and nearness to Allah, mighty and majestic. Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? They are groups from scattered tribes gathering upon the remembrance of Allah, selecting the choicest speech as the eater of dates selects the choicest thereof.""**"
"16772","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, asked about women's gatherings, restricted their good to two occasions and warned of their assemblage with a parable of iron, fire and sparks. > **""There is no good in their gathering except for remembrance or a funeral; their gathering when assembled is like a polisher who puts iron into the fire, and when it burns he strikes it, so its sparks burn all they touch.""**"
"16773","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the spoil of remembrance gatherings and received a doubled answer. > **""What is the spoil of the gatherings of remembrance? The spoil of the gatherings of remembrance is Paradise, Paradise.""**"
"16774","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed a divine address to the son of Adam on mutual remembrance and approaching, with Qatādah remarking on divine swiftness in forgiveness. > **""O son of Adam, if you remember Me within yourself I remember you within Myself; if you remember Me in a company I remember you in a company, among the angels or in a company, better than it; if you draw near Me a span I draw near you a cubit, if you draw near Me a cubit I draw near you a fathom, and if you come to Me walking I come to you hastening.""**"
"16775","Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed a divine promise of reciprocal remembrance in angelic and highest company. > **""No servant remembers Me within himself except I remember him in a company of My angels, and none remembers Me in a company except I remember him in the Highest Companionship.""**"
"16776","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a divine address to the son of Adam on solitary and communal remembrance and its superior return. > **""O son of Adam, when you remember Me alone I remember you alone, and when you remember Me in a company I remember you in a company better than those among whom you remember Me.""**"
"16777","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold the admission of rememberers even from their beds to the highest Gardens. > **""Peoples will surely remember Allah upon well-spread beds, and He will admit them to the highest Gardens.""**"
"16778","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ an interpretation of the verse on the allies of Allah as those whose very mention evokes remembrance. > **""Allah is remembered by their mention.""**"
"16779","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the allies of Allah, he defined them by the effect of their sight. > **""Who are the allies of Allah? Those who, when seen, Allah is remembered.""**"
"16780","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened certain people to keys whose sight evokes remembrance. > **""Among people are keys to the remembrance of Allah; when they are seen, Allah is remembered.""**"
"16781","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the rejoicing and boasting of every spot where Allah is remembered, and the adornment of the earth for desert prayer. > **""No spot upon which Allah is remembered with prayer or remembrance fails to rejoice thereby down to its limit through seven earths, and to boast over the surrounding spots; and no servant stands in a desert land intending prayer except the earth is adorned for him.""**"
"16782","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the boasting and rejoicing of every spot where Allah is remembered in prayer. > **""No spot in which Allah is remembered with prayer fails to boast over the surrounding spots, and to rejoice for the remembrance of Allah down to its limit through seven earths.""**"
"16783","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, states in an athar that mountains call to one another over passers-by who remember Allah and rejoice at affirmation, with ʿAwn observing that they hear good most keenly and reciting the verses on ascribing a son to the Most Merciful."
"16784","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a divine address equating remembrance with gratitude and forgetfulness with denial. > **""O son of Adam, when you remember Me you thank Me, and when you forget Me you deny Me.""**"
"16785","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that neglect of abundant remembrance severs one from faith. > **""Whoever does not increase in the remembrance of Allah, exalted be He, has dissociated from faith.""**"
"16786","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that sittings devoid of remembrance and blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ become regret on the Resurrection Day even for those admitted to Paradise. > **""No people sit in a sitting in which they do not remember Allah, mighty and majestic, nor send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, except it will be a regret upon them on the Day of Resurrection, even if they enter Paradise for the reward.""**"
"16787","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared gatherings abandoned without remembrance and blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ a deficiency upon their people. > **""No people sit in a gathering then rise from it without remembering Allah, nor sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, except that gathering will be a deficiency upon them.""**"
"16788","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold that sittings without remembrance will appear as regret on the Resurrection Day. > **""No people sit in a sitting in which they do not remember Allah therein except they will see it as a regret on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16789","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extended the warning of deficiency to sittings, journeys and beds left without remembrance. > **""No people sit in a sitting without remembering Allah therein except it will be a deficiency upon them; no man walks a path without remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, except it will be a deficiency upon him; and no man retires to his bed without remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, except it will be a deficiency upon him.""**"
"16790","ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold that assemblies dispersing without remembrance will prove regret on the Resurrection Day. > **""No people gather in a sitting then disperse without remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, except that sitting will be a regret upon them on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16791","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn that every hour devoid of good remembrance becomes regret on the Resurrection Day. > **""No hour passes by the son of Adam in which he does not remember Allah therein with good except he will feel regret for it on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16792","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon his Companions recalling their former ignorance, misguidance and divine guidance, approved their discourse with delight, and enjoined constancy therein. > **""You have done well; be thus, and do thus.""**"
"16793","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a parable likening the rememberer among the heedless to the steadfast amid the fleeing. > **""The rememberer of Allah, exalted be He, among the heedless is like the steadfast one among those fleeing.""**"
"16794","ʿIṣmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted the most beloved and most hateful deeds before Allah, and upon inquiry defined both through scenes of conversation and complaint. > **""The most beloved deed to Allah, mighty and majestic, is the glorification within conversation, and the most hateful deed to Allah, glory and exaltation be to Him, is complaining denial. What is the glorification within conversation? That people converse while a man glorifies. What is complaining denial? That people in good are asked by neighbour or companion yet say: we are in evil.""**"
"16795","Saʿd ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commended hidden remembrance and sufficient provision. > **""The best remembrance is the hidden, and the best provision is what suffices.""**"
"16796","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred tooth-stick prayer seventyfold and described hidden remembrance of seventyfold merit with a hidden divine store on the Day of Gathering, when guardians confess their records complete. > **""Hidden remembrance unheard by another carries seventyfold merit; when Allah gathers creation for their reckoning and the guardians bring what they preserved and wrote, Allah says: look, does anything remain for him? They say: our Lord, we omitted nothing we knew and preserved except we counted and wrote it; and Allah, blessed and exalted be He, says to him: you have with Me a hidden store you do not know, and I shall reward you for it, and it is hidden remembrance.""**"
"16797","Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when he sought counsel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined effacing evil with good, and upon inquiry ranked the declaration of divine unity highest. > **""When you do an evil deed, follow it with a good deed to efface it. Is Lā ilāha illā Allāh among good deeds? It is the best of good deeds.""**"
"16798","Yaʿlā ibn Shaddād reports from his father Shaddād ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, with ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, present confirming him, that the Prophet ﷺ, finding no People of the Book among them, ordered the door closed, commanded raised hands with the declaration, paused, praised Allah, recalled his mission, command and promised Paradise, and announced forgiveness. > **""Is there any stranger among you? Raise your hands and say: Lā ilāha illā Allāh. All praise is due to Allah; O Allah, You sent me with this word, commanded me with it, promised me Paradise upon it, and You never break promise. Rejoice, for Allah has forgiven you.""**"
"16799","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded renewal of faith, and upon inquiry prescribed abundant declaration of unity. > **""Renew your faith. How do we renew our faith, O Messenger of Allah? Increase in saying Lā ilāha illā Allāh.""**"
"16800","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged abundant testimony of unity before death intervenes. > **""Increase in testifying that none is worthy of worship except Allah before a barrier comes between you and it.""**"
"16801","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him the keys of Paradise. > **""The keys of Paradise are testifying that none is worthy of worship except Allah.""**"
"16802","Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the dialogue of Mūsā, peace be upon him, seeking a special formula and learning the cosmic weight of unity. > **""O my Lord, teach me something to remember You and supplicate You with. Say, O Mūsā: Lā ilāha illā Allāh. All Your servants say this. Say: Lā ilāha illā Allāh. None is worthy of worship but You; I seek something You single me out with. O Mūsā, were the seven heavens with all who inhabit them besides Me and the seven earths in one scale, and Lā ilāha illā Allāh in a scale, Lā ilāha illā Allāh would outweigh them.""**"
"16803","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised effacement of the register and replacement with good for every utterance of unity by night or day. > **""No servant says Lā ilāha illā Allāh in any hour of night or day except it effaces the evils in the register until an equal measure of good deeds settles therein.""**"
"16804","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the account of the pillar of light before the Throne trembling at unity until assured of the utterer's forgiveness. > **""Allah, blessed and exalted be He, has a pillar of light before the Throne; when a servant says Lā ilāha illā Allāh it trembles, and Allah says: be still. It says: how can I be still while its utterer is unforgiven? He says: I have forgiven him, and it becomes still.""**"
"16805","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the scales, a man tipped toward the Fire, a restraining caller of the Most Merciful, and a rescuing card. > **""Scales will be set on the Day of Resurrection; a man will be brought and placed in one scale, and all counted against him in the other, so it tips against him and he is sent to the Fire; then as he is taken a caller from the Most Merciful cries: do not hasten, do not hasten, for something remains for him; and a card bearing Lā ilāha illā Allāh is brought and placed with the man in the scale until it tips in his favour.""**"
"16806","Maʿqil ibn Yasār, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened all things to locked doors and named the heavenly key. > **""Everything has a key, and the key of the heavens is saying: Lā ilāha illā Allāh.""**"
"16807","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ denied desolation to the folk of unity in grave and gathering, picturing them shaking dust and praising Allah for removed grief. > **""The folk of Lā ilāha illā Allāh will know no desolation in their graves nor at their gathering; as though I see the folk of Lā ilāha illā Allāh shaking dust from their heads, saying: praise belongs to Allah who removed grief from us.""**"
"16808","In another narration from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extended the same assurance to the moments of death and the grave. > **""The folk of Lā ilāha illā Allāh will know no desolation at death nor at the grave.""**"
"16809","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah have mercy on him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ questioned a man over a deed, heard his solemn denial by the One besides whom none is worthy of worship, affirmed the deed, yet announced forgiveness through sincerity. > **""Did you do such-and-such? No, by the One besides whom none is worthy of worship, O Messenger of Allah, I did not. Yes, you have done it, but you have been forgiven through sincerity.""**"
"16810","In another narration from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, informed the Prophet ﷺ of the deed and its pardon through the word of unity. > **""He has done it, but he has been forgiven for saying: Lā ilāha illā Allāh.""**"
"16811","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when two disputants came to the Prophet ﷺ and one swore falsely by Allah besides whom none is worthy of worship, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, exposed the lie, ordered restitution, and named the expiation. > **""He lies; he owes him a due, so order him to pay him, and the expiation of his oath is his knowledge of Lā ilāha illā Allāh, or his testimony to it.""**"
"16812","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeatedly questioned a man who swore solemnly though the Prophet ﷺ knew the deed, then declared him expiated through affirmation of unity. > **""O so-and-so, did you do such-and-such? No, by the One besides whom none is worthy of worship, I did not. You have been expiated through affirming Lā ilāha illā Allāh.""**"
"16813","Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ in an athar-like narration that a man who swore falsely by Allah besides whom none is worthy of worship was forgiven."
"16814","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when a man confessed every manner of sin, the Prophet ﷺ thrice tested his testimony to unity and to Muḥammad's messengership, and upon assent declared it covering. > **""Do you not testify that none is worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah? That covers that.""**"
"16815","Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he with his people received glad tidings of Paradise for truthful testimony, went out proclaiming them, were returned by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, fearing reliance, and the Prophet ﷺ fell silent. > **""Rejoice and bear glad tidings to those behind you that whoever testifies truthfully that none is worthy of worship except Allah will enter Paradise. O Messenger of Allah, then people will rely. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell silent.""**"
"16816","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the twofold counsel and twofold prohibition of Nūḥ to his son, and when asked about food gatherings and clean dress, defined arrogance as contempt for truth and people. > **""Shall I not inform you of the counsel of Nūḥ to his son? O my son, I enjoin upon you two things and forbid you two: I enjoin upon you saying Lā ilāha illā Allāh, for were it placed in the scale with the heavens and earth it would outweigh them, and were it a ring it would shatter them until it reaches Allah, and saying Glory be to Allah the Mighty and with His praise, for it is the worship of creation and by it their provisions are apportioned; and I forbid you association and arrogance, for they veil from Allah. Is it arrogance for a man to take food with company, or to wear clean clothes? No; arrogance is scorning truth and despising people.""**"
"16817","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ranked unity highest among remembrances and seeking forgiveness highest among supplications, then recited the verse commanding knowledge of unity and seeking pardon. > **""No remembrance is better than Lā ilāha illā Allāh, and no supplication is better than: I ask Allah's forgiveness.""**"
"16818","Abū Ayyūb, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the reward of freeing necks to whoever utters the full word of unity with sovereignty, praise and power, with a narrator's doubt between ten and one. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, it will be for him as the equivalent of ten necks, or a neck.""**"
"16819","Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports also that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the same word of unity to freeing one or two souls, with doubt in the number. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, it will be for him as the equivalent of one freed soul, or two.""**"
"16820","Al-Rabīʿ ibn Khaytham reports the reward of four freed souls among the children of Ismāʿīl, peace be upon him, for an extended word of unity affirming giving life and death, and when questioned traced his hearing through ʿAmr ibn Maymūn and Ibn Abī Laylā to Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, He gives life and causes death, and He has power over all things, it is as one who freed four souls among the children of Ismāʿīl.""**"
"16821","In another narration, the same reward is reported with doubt both in the number of utterances and the number of necks, whether once equalling one neck or ten times equalling ten necks."
"16822","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened granting silver, milk, or guidance on a path to freeing a soul, and likewise the uttered word of unity. > **""Whoever grants a gift of silver, or a gift of milk, or guides along a path, it is as freeing a soul; and whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, it is as freeing a soul.""**"
"16823","In another narration from al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fixed the reward of ten utterances of the word of unity as a freed neck or soul, with doubt in the term. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, ten times, it will be for him as freeing a neck, or a soul.""**"
"16824","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that no deed outstrips the utterer of the word of unity and no sin remains with it. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, no deed outstrips it and no sin remains with it.""**"
"16825","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ promise admission to the Gardens of Bliss for whoever utters an extended word of unity affirming the Ever-Living Who never dies and all good in His Hand, seeking only Allah's Face. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He is the Ever-Living Who never dies, in Whose Hand is good, and He has power over all things, seeking only the Face of Allah thereby, He will admit him thereby into the Gardens of Bliss.""**"
"16826","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise recorded good deeds in unstated number for the brief word of unity without partner. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, there will be recorded for him such-and-such good deeds.""**"
"16827","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised two million good deeds for an extended word affirming the One, the Eternal, Who neither begets nor is born and has no equal. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, One, Eternal, Who neither begets nor is born, and there is none equal to Him, Allah will record for him two million good deeds.""**"
"16828","Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught a testimony calling Allah, His angels, bearers of His Throne and those in the heavens to witness divine unity and Muḥammad's servitude and messengership, with graded freedom from the Fire for one, two and three utterances. > **""O Allah, I call You to witness, and I call Your angels, the bearers of Your Throne, and those in the heavens to witness that You are Allah, none is worthy of worship but You, alone without partner, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is Your servant and Messenger; whoever says it once, Allah frees a third of him from the Fire, whoever says it twice frees two thirds of him from the Fire, and whoever says it thrice is wholly freed from the Fire.""**"
"16829","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that whoever utters the word of unity two hundred times in a day cannot be outstripped by those before nor reached by those after, except one who brings better. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, two hundred times in a day, none before him will have outstripped him and none after him will reach him, except one who brings better than his deed.""**"
"16830","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever says the declaration of unity a hundred times will be raised with a moonlit face and unmatched deed except by equal or greater utterance. > **""No servant says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, a hundred times, except Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection with his face like the moon on the night of fullness, and no deed better than his will be raised that day except one who said the like or more.""**"
"16831","Abū al-Mundhir al-Juhanī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when he asked the Prophet ﷺ for the best speech, he was taught an extended word of unity with life, death and good in His Hand a hundred times each day as the best deed of the day. > **""O Abā al-Mundhir, say: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, He gives life and causes death, in His Hand is good, and He has power over all things, a hundred times each day, for on that day you will be the best of people in deed, except one who said the like of what you said.""**"
"16832","Muʿādh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Rāfiʿ reports that in a gathering with Ibn ʿUmar, Ibn Jaʿfar and Ibn Abī ʿAmrah, the last conveyed Muʿādh ibn Jabal's hearing from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of two words, one unbarred to the Throne and one filling heaven and earth; Ibn ʿUmar confirmed the hearing, wept till his beard was dyed with tears, and declared attachment to both. > **""Two words, one of which has no barrier short of the Throne, and the other fills what is between heaven and earth: none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest; they are two words we cling to and cherish.""**"
"16833","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ graded freedom from the Fire for uttering the declaration with magnification once, twice and four times. > **""Whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest, Allah frees a quarter of him from the Fire; none says it twice except Allah frees half of him from the Fire; and if he says it four times Allah frees him from the Fire.""**"
"16834","Salmān of Islam, meaning al-Fārisī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ taught an expanded testimony invoking angels, Throne-bearers, heavens, earths and all creation, disavowing deniers, with graded freedom from the Fire. > **""O Allah, I call You to witness, and I call Your angels, the bearers of Your Throne, the heavens and those therein, the earths and those therein, and I call all Your creation to witness that You are Allah, none is worthy of worship but You, and I disavow whoever among the first and last refuses that, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is Your servant and Messenger; whoever says it, Allah frees a third of him from the Fire, whoever says it twice Allah frees two thirds of him from the Fire, and whoever says it thrice is freed from the Fire.""**"
"16835","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ a hundred and twenty-four thousand good deeds for glorification with praise, and a covenant with Allah on the Resurrection Day for the declaration of unity. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah and with His praise, a hundred thousand good deeds and twenty-four thousand good deeds will be recorded for him; and whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, it will be for him a covenant with Allah on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16836","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the command to increase in the lasting righteous deeds, and upon inquiry their identification as magnification, declaration, praise, glorification and the hawqalah. > **""Increase in the lasting righteous deeds. What are they, O Messenger of Allah? Magnification, declaration of unity, praise, glorification, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16837","Abū Saʿīd and Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named four chosen words with twentyfold reward and remission each, and thirtyfold for praise of the Lord of the Worlds uttered from oneself. > **""Allah has chosen four from speech: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest; whoever says: glory be to Allah, twenty good deeds are recorded for him and twenty evils removed, and likewise for Allah is Greatest and for none is worthy of worship except Allah; and whoever says: praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the Worlds, from himself, thirty good deeds are recorded for him and thirty evils removed.""**"
"16838","In another narration the same rewards are stated without doubt, with praise and magnification from oneself carrying thirtyfold reward and remission. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah, twenty good deeds are recorded for him and twenty evils removed; whoever says: praise belongs to Allah, the like; whoever says: none is worthy of worship except Allah, the like; and whoever says: Allah is Greatest from himself, thirty good deeds are recorded for him and thirty evils removed.""**"
"16839","A man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the best speech consists of four words of glorification, praise, unity and magnification. > **""The best speech is: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest.""**"
"16840","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named four words as the best speech after the Qurʾān, themselves part of it, in any order. > **""The best speech after the Qurʾān, and they are part of the Qurʾān, is four, and it harms you not with which you begin: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest.""**"
"16841","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ four chosen words, not themselves Qurʾān yet drawn from it. > **""Allah has chosen four from speech, not Qurʾān yet part of the Qurʾān: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest.""**"
"16842","Abū al-Mundhir al-Juhanī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the fuller teaching of a hundredfold extended unity as the best deed, with frequent glorification, praise, unity and hawqalah as master of seeking forgiveness, effacement of sins and, as thought, necessitating Paradise. > **""O Abā al-Mundhir, say: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, to Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs praise, He gives life and causes death, in His Hand is good, and He has power over all things, a hundred times each day, for on that day you will be the best of people in deed except one who said the like of what you said; and increase in saying: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, for it is the master of seeking forgiveness, an effacement of sins, and, I think he said, necessitating Paradise.""**"
"16843","A freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exclaimed over five things how heavy they are on the scale: the four words and a righteous child taken with patience by his father. > **""Glad tidings, glad tidings, for five, how heavy they are on the scale: none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, and a righteous child taken and borne with patience by his father.""**"
"16844","Thawbān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exclaimed over five heavy things on the scale including a righteous child dying and borne with patience. > **""Glad tidings, glad tidings, for five, how heavy they are on the scale: none is worthy of worship except Allah, glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, Allah is Greatest, and a righteous child dying and borne with patience by a man.""**"
"16845","Abū Salmā, the shepherd of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exclaim over five heavy things including glorification with praise and a righteous child taken from a Muslim and borne with patience. > **""Glad tidings, glad tidings, for five, how heavy they are on the scale: none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, glory be to Allah and with His praise, and a righteous child taken from a Muslim man and borne with patience by him.""**"
"16846","Safīnah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exclaimed over five heavy things including a righteous predecessor child granted to a man. > **""Glad tidings, glad tidings, for five, how heavy they are on the scale: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and a righteous predecessor granted to a man.""**"
"16847","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ told his sitters to take their shield, and when they asked in parental ransom whether an enemy had come, explained the shield from the Fire as five words, foremost, saving, following and lasting righteous. > **""Take your shield. With our father and mother as ransom, O Messenger of Allah, has an enemy come? Take your shield from the Fire; say: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, for they are foremost, saving, following, and the lasting righteous deeds.""**"
"16848","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out commanding the shield, and when asked about a present enemy, explained the shield from the Fire as five words coming on the Resurrection Day foremost, saving, shielding and lasting righteous. > **""Take your shield. O Messenger of Allah, from a present enemy? Take your shield from the Fire; say: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, for they will come on the Day of Resurrection foremost, saving, shielding, and the lasting righteous deeds.""**"
"16849","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described an angel sealing the five words with his wing and bearing them to the Throne, with blessings upon them and their utterer, glorification as Allah's absolution from all evil, and the hawqalah as the servant's submission. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, an angel seals them with his wing and does not cease until he bears them to the Throne; they pass nothing except it blesses them and their utterer; glorification is Allah's absolution from all evil; and whoever says: there is no power nor strength except through Allah, the Exalted, the Mighty, He says: My servant has submitted and surrendered.""**"
"16850","Al-Ḥārith, freedman of ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports in an athar that ʿUthmān sat with them, the caller to prayer came, he spoke of obligatory prayer effacing sins as good deeds removing evils, and when asked what the lasting deeds are, listed six words of unity, glorification, praise, magnification and hawqalah. > **""They are the good deeds removing evils. What are the lasting deeds, O ʿUthmān? None is worthy of worship except Allah, glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16851","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, states in a stopped athar that for every glorification, praise or magnification Allah plants a tree in Paradise of gold root, jewel top, crowned with pearl and ruby, its fruits like young maidens' bosoms, softer than butter and sweeter than honey, ever replaced, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is reported to have recited the verse on unfailing bounty. > **""No servant glorifies Allah with a glorification, praises Him with a praise, or magnifies Him with a magnification, except Allah plants for him thereby a tree in Paradise, its root of gold, its top of jewel, crowned with pearl and ruby, its fruits like the bosoms of young maidens, softer than butter and sweeter than honey; whenever anything is gathered from it, its place is restored.""**"
"16852","Salmān, meaning al-Fārisī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe Paradise's hollows and command plentiful planting, and upon inquiry named the four words as its planting. > **""In Paradise are hollows, so plant them plentifully. O Messenger of Allah, what is their planting? Glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest.""**"
"16853","Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise a Paradisal tree of red ruby root crowned with pearl for each glorification, praise, declaration and magnification. > **""Whoever glorifies Allah with a glorification, praises Him with a praise, declares His unity with a declaration, and magnifies Him with a magnification, Allah plants for him a tree in Paradise, its root red ruby, crowned with pearl, its spathes like the bosoms of young maidens, sweeter than honey and softer than butter.""**"
"16854","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, states in a stopped athar that Allah apportioned characters as provisions, grants wealth to loved and unloved but faith only to the beloved, and enjoins the miserly, cowardly and night-weary to increase in four words."
"16855","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded five words as the lasting righteous deeds that shed sins as trees shed leaves and belong to Paradise's treasures. > **""Say: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, for they are the lasting righteous deeds, and they shed sins as the tree sheds its leaves, and they are among the treasures of Paradise.""**"
"16856","In another narration from Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ added the command to take those lasting words before a barrier intervenes. > **""Take them before a barrier intervenes between you and them, the lasting deeds.""**"
"16857","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ enjoined five words of glorification, praise, unity, magnification and hawqalah. > **""Hold to these five: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16858","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, states in an athar that when he narrates a ḥadīth he brings its confirmation from Allah's Book, and describes an angel grasping five words including the Blessed Name, bearing them under his wing past angelic hosts seeking forgiveness until the Face of the Most Merciful, then reciting the verse on the ascent of good words. > **""When the Muslim servant says: praise belongs to Allah, glory be to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and blessed be Allah, an angel grasps them, places them under his wing, ascends with them past no company of angels except they seek forgiveness for their utterer, until he brings them to the Face of the Most Merciful.""**"
"16859","ʿImrān, meaning Ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether each of them could daily do a deed like Uḥud, and upon their wonder affirmed all can, naming each of the four words greater than Uḥud. > **""Can none of you do each day a deed like Uḥud? O Messenger of Allah, who can do each day a deed like Uḥud? Each of you can. What is it? Glory be to Allah is greater than Uḥud, praise belongs to Allah is greater than Uḥud, none is worthy of worship except Allah is greater than Uḥud, and Allah is Greatest is greater than Uḥud.""**"
"16860","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise ten good deeds per letter of the four words, then warn against aiding false disputes, blocking penalties by intercession, and slandering believers with confinement in the filth of wounds. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest, ten good deeds are recorded for him for every letter; and whoever aids a false dispute remains in Allah's wrath until he desists, whoever blocks a penalty of Allah's penalties by his intercession opposes Allah in His command, and whoever slanders a believing man or woman, Allah will confine him on the Resurrection Day in the filth of wounds until he exits what he said, and he will never exit.""**"
"16861","Saʿd, meaning Ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a Bedouin asked the Prophet ﷺ for words to say and was taught an extended formula of unity, supreme magnification, universal glorification and the mighty hawqalah. > **""Say: none is worthy of worship except Allah, alone without partner, Allah is Greatest in greatness, glory be to Allah, Lord of the Worlds, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, the Exalted, the Mighty.""**"
"16862","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invited Abū Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, to graze and rest in Paradise's meadow, and upon inquiry named four words; Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, likewise named them as Paradise's plantings when asked of every thing's planting. > **""Will you not graze in the meadow of Paradise and rest therein? O Messenger of Allah, what is grazing? Praise belongs to Allah, glory be to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest; everything has a planting, so what is the planting of Paradise? Glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest.""**"
"16863","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw Ibrāhīm the Friend, peace be upon him, on the night of his ascension, who bade convey greeting to his nation and describe Paradise's pure soil, sweet water and hollows with its five-word plantings. > **""O Muḥammad, convey my greeting to your nation and inform them that Paradise is pure of soil, sweet of water, and hollows, and its plantings are: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16864","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised a tree in Paradise for each of the four words. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, none is worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is Greatest, a tree will be planted for him in Paradise for each one of them.""**"
"16865","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened a hundred glorifications with praise to a hundred sacrificial camels, a hundred praises to a hundred bridled steeds in Allah's path, and a hundred magnifications to a hundred camels slaughtered at Makkah. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah and with His praise, a hundred times, it is as a hundred sacrificial camels; whoever says: praise belongs to Allah, a hundred times, it equals a hundred bridled saddled horses in the path of Allah; and whoever says: Allah is Greatest, a hundred times, it is as a hundred camels slaughtered at Makkah.""**"
"16866","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Umm Hāniʾ asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a heavy woman for beneficial supplications, and was taught hundredfold glorification equalling freed necks, praise equalling steeds, magnification equalling gifted camels, and unity after which no sin overtakes short of association. > **""Say: glory be to Allah, a hundred times, equalling a hundred necks freed for Allah, mighty and majestic; praise Allah, a hundred times, equalling a hundred bridled horses borne in the path of Allah; magnify Allah, a hundred times, equalling a hundred garlanded camels gifted to the House of Allah; and declare Allah's unity, a hundred times, and no sin will overtake you short of association.""**"
"16867","Umm Hāniʾ daughter of Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by her and, upon her plea of age and weakness for a deed performed sitting, taught hundredfold glorification, praise, magnification and declaration with rewards of Ismāʿīlī necks, steeds, accepted camels and heaven-earth fullness unmatched except by equal or more, with a variant plea for Paradise and the declaration best under heaven and earth. > **""Glorify Allah a hundred glorifications, equalling a hundred necks you free among the children of Ismāʿīl; praise Allah a hundred praises, equalling a hundred saddled bridled horses you bear in the path of Allah; magnify Allah a hundred magnifications, equalling a hundred garlanded accepted camels; and declare Allah's unity a hundred times, filling what is between heaven and earth, and no deed will be raised that day for anyone except one who brings the like of what you brought or more.""**"
"16868","Salmā, mother of Abī Rāfiʿ's children, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that when she asked for few words, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught tenfold magnification and glorification each answered by Allah as His own, and tenfold pleas for forgiveness each answered as done. > **""Say: Allah is Greatest, ten times; Allah says: this is Mine; and say: glory be to Allah, ten times; Allah says: this is Mine; and say: O Allah, forgive me; He says: I have done so; you say it ten times and He says: I have done so.""**"
"16869","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ that Jibrīl placed his hands upon his chest and between his shoulders till each cold was felt opposite, then commanded magnifying the Great, declaring with certainty, and glorifying the Lord of first and last. > **""O Muḥammad, magnify the Great, declare with certainty, and say: glory be to the Lord of the first and the last.""**"
"16870","Abū Umāmah, meaning al-Bāhilī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out while he moved his lips in remembrance, and upon learning so, taught a multiplied praise, glorification and magnification by Book, creation, heavens and earth, and all things, surpassing night-and-day devotion. > **""With what do you move your lips? I remember Allah, O Messenger of Allah. Shall I not inform you of something which, when you say it then persist night and day, you cannot reach? Say: praise belongs to Allah as many as His Book counted, praise belongs to Allah as many as are in His Book, praise belongs to Allah as many as He counted of His creation, praise belongs to Allah filling His creation, praise belongs to Allah filling His heavens and earth, praise belongs to Allah as many as all things, and praise belongs to Allah for all things; and glorify likewise and magnify likewise.""**"
"16871","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports also that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him moving his lips, and upon learning he remembered Allah, taught an even greater praise and glorification by creation, heavens, earth, Book and all things, commanding transmission to descendants. > **""What do you say, O Abā Umāmah? I remember Allah. Shall I not guide you to what is greater than remembrance of night over day? Say: praise belongs to Allah as many as He created, praise belongs to Allah filling what He created, praise belongs to Allah as many as are in the heavens and earth, praise belongs to Allah filling what His Book counted, praise belongs to Allah as many as He counted of all things; and glorify Allah likewise. Learn them and teach them to your descendants after you.""**"
"16872","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ multiplied praise and glorification by Allah's creation, Book, all things and their fullness. > **""Praise belongs to Allah as many as Allah created, praise belongs to Allah as many as His Book counted, praise belongs to Allah as many as all things, praise belongs to Allah filling all things; and glory be to Allah filling all things, and glory be to Allah as many as all things.""**"
"16873","Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd reports that Abū Umāmah narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ multiplied praise by creation, heavens, earth, Book and all things with like glorification, enjoining its magnification. > **""Praise belongs to Allah as many as He created, praise belongs to Allah filling what He created, praise belongs to Allah as many as are in the heavens and earth, praise belongs to Allah filling what is in the heavens and earth, praise belongs to Allah as many as His Book counted, praise belongs to Allah filling what His Book counted, praise belongs to Allah as many as all things, praise belongs to Allah filling all things, and glory be to Allah likewise; so magnify that greatly.""**"
"16874","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him moving his lips, and upon learning he remembered Allah, taught a glorification and praise by creation, all things and Book-count as better than night-with-day remembrance. > **""O Abā al-Dardāʾ, what do you say? I remember Allah. Shall I not teach you what is better than remembrance of Allah by night with day and day with night? Say: glory be to Allah as many as He created, glory be to Allah as many as all things, glory be to Allah filling what His Book counted; and praise belongs to Allah as many as He created, praise belongs to Allah filling what He created, and praise belongs to Allah filling what His Book counted.""**"
"16875","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised a palm tree planted in Paradise for glorification with praise. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah and with His praise, a palm tree will be planted for him in Paradise.""**"
"16876","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined the night-burdened, miserly and cowardly to increase in glorification with praise as dearer to Allah than a mountain of gold spent in His path. > **""Whoever is burdened by the night to endure it, or miserly with wealth to spend it, or cowardly toward the enemy to fight him, let him increase in: glory be to Allah and with His praise, for it is dearer to Allah than a mountain of gold spent in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic.""**"
"16877","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged each man not to forgo two thousand good deeds each morning through a hundred glorifications with praise, covering like sins while other good remains ample. > **""Let no man of you forgo doing for Allah each day two thousand good deeds; when he wakes say: glory be to Allah and with His praise, a hundred times, for they are two thousand good deeds, and though he commit like sins that day, whatever other good he did remains ample.""**"
"16878","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described glorification with the Mighty and repentance as recorded verbatim, hung upon the Throne, unerased by sins until sealed meeting with Allah. > **""Glory be to Allah and with His praise, glory be to Allah the Mighty, I ask Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him; whoever says it, it is recorded as he said it, then hung upon the Throne, no sin of its utterer effacing it until he meets Allah on the Day of Resurrection sealed as he said it.""**"
"16879","Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that every morning a crier calls creation to glorify the Holy King. > **""No morning dawns upon the servants except a crier cries: O creation, glorify the Holy King.""**"
"16880","Ṭalḥah, meaning Ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the meaning of glory be to Allah, and was told it is Allah's absolution from evil. > **""Absolution of Allah, blessed and exalted be He, from evil.""**"
"16881","Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a Paradisal planting for whoever glorifies Allah the Mighty. > **""Whoever says: glory be to Allah the Mighty, a planting will sprout for him in Paradise.""**"
"16882","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marvelled at Allah's decree for the believer, rewarded in prosperity with praise and gratitude and in calamity with praise and patience, in everything even a morsel raised to the mouth or to his wife's mouth. > **""I marvel at Allah's decree, mighty and majestic, for the believer: if good befalls him he praises his Lord and thanks, and if calamity befalls him he praises his Lord and endures; the believer is rewarded in everything, even in a morsel he raises to his mouth.""**"
"16883","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the first called to Paradise those who praise Allah in ease and hardship. > **""The first to be called to Paradise are the praisers, those who praise Allah in ease and hardship.""**"
"16884","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the best servants of Allah, exalted be He, on the Resurrection Day the praisers."
"16885","Muṭarrif reports in an athar that ʿImrān, may Allah be pleased with him, told him he narrates today a ḥadīth by which Allah may benefit him after today: the best servants of Allah on the Resurrection Day are the praisers."
"16886","Al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ declaring he had praised his Lord and the Prophet ﷺ with praises and eulogy, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered that his Lord loves eulogy, in one version praise. > **""Your Lord, blessed and exalted be He, loves eulogy.""**"
"16887","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that no blessing for which the servant praises Allah fails to be surpassed by that praise, however great. > **""No blessing Allah, mighty and majestic, bestows upon a servant for which he praises Allah, mighty and majestic, except it is better than that blessing, however great.""**"
"16888","Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ telling how, while praying, he heard a speaker utter an all-encompassing praise, kingship, goodness, return, worthiness and a plea for past forgiveness, future protection and accepted deeds, and the Prophet ﷺ identified him as an angel teaching praise of his Lord. > **""O Allah, to You belongs all praise, to You belongs all sovereignty, in Your Hand is all good, to You returns all matter, its open and secret; worthy are You to be praised; You have power over all things; O Allah, forgive me all my past sins, protect me in what remains of my life, and grant me a pure deed with which You are pleased with me. That is an angel who came to teach you the praise of your Lord, mighty and majestic.""**"
"16889","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ convey that Allah says His believing servant ranks with Him as all good, praising Him even as He draws his soul from between his sides. > **""My believing servant ranks with Me as all good: he praises Me while I draw his soul from between his sides.""**"
"16890","Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the verse of might is the praise of Allah Who took no child and has no partner in sovereignty to the end of the chapter. > **""The verse of might is: praise belongs to Allah Who took no child and has no partner in sovereignty, to the end of the chapter.""**"
"16891","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise a thousand good deeds, a thousand ranks and seventy thousand angels seeking forgiveness till the Resurrection for whoever seeks Allah's bounty through fourfold praise of His subduing greatness, might, sovereignty and power. > **""Whoever says: praise belongs to Allah before Whose greatness all things humble themselves, praise belongs to Allah before Whose might all things are abased, praise belongs to Allah before Whose sovereignty all things bow, and praise belongs to Allah before Whose power all things submit, seeking thereby what is with Allah, Allah will record for him thereby a thousand good deeds, raise him thereby a thousand ranks, and appoint thereby seventy thousand angels seeking forgiveness for him till the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16892","Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told how a man said abundant praise, the angel deemed it too great to record, referred it to his Lord, and was told to record it as His servant abundantly said it. > **""A man said: praise belongs to Allah abundantly; the angel deemed it too great to record, referred it to his Lord, mighty and majestic, and it was said to him: record it as My servant said it, abundantly.""**"
"16893","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man beside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ uttered a phrase of praise, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked who had spoken it, the man fell silent fearing rebuke, until the Prophet ﷺ reassured him that he had spoken only what was right, whereupon the man acknowledged his words and his hope for good through them. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I saw thirteen angels racing toward your words, to see which of them would raise them to Allah, the Blessed and Exalted.""**"
"16894","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he sat with the Prophet ﷺ in a gathering when a man greeted the Prophet ﷺ and the people, received the full return of greeting, then sat and praised Allah with an extended formula, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked him to repeat his words, the man did so. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, ten angels raced toward it, each of them keen to record it, yet they did not know how to write it until they raised it to the Possessor of Might, Who said: Write it as My servant spoke it.""**"
"16895","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the reward for a threefold formula of praise. > **""Whoever says: Praise belongs to Allah before all, praise belongs to Allah after all, and praise belongs to Allah in every state, is granted a reward like the worship of one who worshipped Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"16896","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Jibril, peace be upon him, descended upon him and addressed him by name, offering a formula of praise for one who desires to worship Allah for a night with worship as it deserves. > **""O Allah, to You belongs praise, eternal with Your eternity, and to You belongs praise, perpetual, with no end but Your will, at every glance of the eye and every breath of the soul.""**"
"16897","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to guide him to a gate among the gates of Paradise, and when Muadh asked what it was, the Prophet ﷺ named it. > **""Shall I not guide you to a gate among the gates of Paradise? There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16898","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the night the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken by night, he passed by Ibrahim, peace be upon him, who asked Jibril who was with him, and when told it was Muhammad ﷺ, Ibrahim greeted him, welcomed him, and urged him to command his nation to increase the plantings of Paradise, describing its pure soil and vast land, and naming the plantings when asked. > **""Command your nation to increase the plantings of Paradise, for its soil is pure and its land is vast; and the plantings of Paradise are: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16899","Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas reported that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, offered to teach him a phrase the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had taught him during the Prophet stay in his house, when the Prophet ﷺ had offered Abu Ayyub a phrase from the treasure of Paradise and Abu Ayyub had eagerly accepted with devotion to the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Shall I not teach you, O Abu Ayyub, a phrase from the treasure of Paradise? Say frequently: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16900","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged increase of the plantings of Paradise and described its sweet water and pure soil. > **""Increase the plantings of Paradise, for its water is sweet and its soil is pure; so increase its plantings: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16901","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the curative effect of the treasure phrase, naming the lightest of the ailments. > **""There is no power nor strength except through Allah is a remedy for ninety-nine ailments, the lightest of which is anxiety.""**"
"16902","Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ offered to guide his people to a treasure among the treasures of Paradise and commanded frequent repetition of its phrase. > **""Shall I not guide you to a treasure among the treasures of Paradise? Say frequently: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16903","Zayd ibn Ishaq al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met him at the door of the mosque, offered to guide him to a treasure among the treasures of Paradise, and named it when Zayd eagerly accepted. > **""Shall I not guide you to a treasure among the treasures of Paradise? There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16904","Muawiya ibn Hayda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the rank of the treasure phrase. > **""There is no power nor strength except through Allah is a treasure among the treasures of Paradise.""**"
"16905","Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubada, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that one day, after he had prayed the dawn prayer and lain down, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ nudged him with his foot, offered to guide him to a treasure among the treasures of Paradise, and named it. > **""Shall I not guide you to a treasure among the treasures of Paradise? There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16906","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the garden walls of Madina, the Prophet ﷺ called him, and when he responded, the Prophet ﷺ warned that the greatest hoarders of wealth will be the least on the Day of Resurrection except one who spends thus and thus, gesturing right and left, and noted how few they are. He then offered Abu Hurayra a treasure of Paradise and asked him of the mutual rights between Allah and His servants, explaining them when Abu Hurayra deferred to Allah and His Messenger. > **""There is no power nor strength except through Allah, and there is no refuge from Allah except in Him. The right of Allah over the servants is that they worship Him without associating anything with Him, and the right of the servants upon Allah is that He not punish one who associates nothing with Him.""**"
"16907","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was with the Prophet ﷺ when he uttered the treasure phrase, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he knew its explanation, he deferred to Allah and His Messenger, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ explained it. > **""There is no turning away from disobedience to Allah except through the protection of Allah, and no strength for obedience to Allah except through the help of Allah.""**"
"16908","Fadala ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bound the quest for the treasure of Paradise to a single phrase. > **""Whoever desires the treasure of Paradise must hold fast to: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16909","Uqba ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked the preservation of a blessing to frequent repetition of the treasure phrase and then recited the verse of the garden from the chapter of the Cave. > **""Whoever is blessed by Allah with a blessing and wishes for it to remain should say frequently: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16910","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ offered to guide him to a phrase from the treasure of Paradise beneath the Throne, and when Abu Hurayra answered with devotion, the Prophet ﷺ named the phrase. The narrator Abu Balj added his recollection that Allah says of its speaker that His servant has submitted and surrendered, and when questioned about the wording, Abu Hurayra pointed to the verse of the chapter of the Cave. > **""Shall I not guide you to a phrase, a treasure from the treasure of Paradise beneath the Throne? Say: There is no power nor strength except through Allah, for Allah says: My servant has submitted and surrendered.""**"
"16911","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Fatima to him, her dowry consisted of a woolen garment, a leather pillow stuffed with fiber, two millstones, a waterskin, and two jars. One day Ali complained to Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, of his pained chest from drawing water and urged her to ask her father for a servant since captives had come to him, and Fatima told of her blistered hands from grinding. She went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked what had brought her, O his daughter, but she said only that she had come to greet him and returned out of shyness. When Ali asked what she had done, she said she was too shy to ask, so they went together to the Prophet ﷺ and laid their complaints before him, asking for a servant from the captives and ease Allah had granted him. The Prophet ﷺ swore he would not grant them while the people of the Bench lay hungry without provision, but would sell the captives and spend their price upon them. They returned, and the Prophet ﷺ later came to them as they lay under a small blanket too short to cover heads and feet together; as they rose he told them to stay in place, offered them something better than they had asked, and when they accepted, taught them phrases Jibril had taught him for after every prayer and at bedtime. > **""Shall I not inform you of something better than what you asked me? Glorify Allah ten times, praise Him ten times, and magnify Him ten times after every prayer; and when you retire to bed, glorify Allah thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and magnify Him thirty-four times.""**"
"16912","Umm al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that a man stayed with Abu al-Darda, who asked whether he was settling or traveling, and when the man said he was traveling, Abu al-Darda promised him journey-provision, declaring he would have given better had he known any. He then recounted his complaint to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the wealthy had carried off this world and the next despite shared prayers and fasts, since only they could give charity, and the Prophet answer guiding him to unmatched words after every prayer. > **""Shall I not guide you to something by which none before you can outstrip you and none after you can reach you, except one who does the same? After every prayer say thirty-three glorifications, thirty-three praises, and thirty-four magnifications of Allah.""**"
"16913","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when people came conversing with him, he told them he would provision them with what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had provisioned him for after every prayer, closing with the declaration of divine oneness. > **""When you pray, glorify Allah thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and magnify Him thirty-four times after every prayer, and say: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things.""**"
"16914","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ the commanded count of glorification, praise, and magnification after the prayers. > **""We were commanded to glorify Allah after the prayers with thirty-three glorifications, thirty-three praises, and thirty-four magnifications.""**"
"16915","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness even for sea-foam-like sins to one who completes the ninety-nine with the hundredth declaration of oneness after every prayer. > **""Whoever glorifies Allah thirty-three times, praises Him thirty-three times, and magnifies Him thirty-three times after every prayer, then completes the hundred with: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, will have his sins forgiven even if they are like the foam of the sea.""**"
"16916","Abu Kathir, freedman of the Banu Hashim, reported that he heard Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, describe phrases remembered a hundred times after every prayer that efface sins even if like the foam of the sea. > **""Allah is Greatest, glory belongs to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, and there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"16917","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the poor believers complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that their wealthy brothers shared their affirmation, faith, and fasting yet surpassed them through wealth spent in charity, kinship, and the path of Allah, while they as needy people could not. The Prophet ﷺ guided them to elevenfold phrases after every prayer by which they would reach the same merit, and they acted upon it and mentioned it to the wealthy, who adopted it too. When the poor returned complaining that their brothers now said the same, the Prophet ﷺ ascribed it to the bounty of Allah granted to whom He wills and gave the company of the poor glad tidings of entering Paradise half a day, five hundred years, before the wealthy. The narrator Musa ibn Ubayda then recited the verse that a day with the Lord is like a thousand years of human reckoning. > **""Shall I not inform you of something by which you will reach their merit? Say after every prayer: Allah is Greatest eleven times, praise belongs to Allah likewise, there is no god but Allah likewise, and glory belongs to Allah likewise. And receive glad tidings, O company of the poor: the poor Muslims will enter Paradise half a day, five hundred years, before their wealthy.""**"
"16918","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw Umm Sulaym praying in her house and directed her to tenfold glorification, praise, and magnification after the obligatory prayer, promising a threefold divine response to whatever she then asks. > **""O Umm Sulaym, when you have prayed the obligatory prayer, say: Glory belongs to Allah ten times, praise belongs to Allah ten times, and Allah is Greatest ten times; then ask whatever you wish, for He will say to you: Yes, yes, yes.""**"
"16919","Umm Malik al-Ansariya, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she brought a vessel of clarified butter to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who ordered Bilal to press it out and returned it to her, yet she found it full upon returning home. She came back asking whether anything had been revealed concerning her and why her gift had been returned, so the Prophet ﷺ questioned Bilal, who swore he had pressed it until he felt shy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ congratulated Umm Malik for a blessing whose reward Allah had hastened and taught her tenfold phrases after every prayer. > **""Congratulations to you, O Umm Malik; this is a blessing whose reward Allah has hastened. After every prayer say: Glory belongs to Allah ten times, praise belongs to Allah ten times, and Allah is Greatest ten times.""**"
"16920","Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utba reported that a man prayed beside Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As and heard him say a formula of peace upon giving the salam, then prayed beside Abdullah ibn Umar and heard the same, so he laughed. When Ibn Umar asked what made him laugh, the man explained, and Ibn Umar answered that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say it. > **""O Allah, You are Peace and from You comes peace; blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor.""**"
"16921","Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl reported that the early people deemed it good for a man finishing his prayer to utter a formula of peace and blessing. > **""O Allah, You are Peace and from You comes peace; blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor.""**"
"16922","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied the recitation of the Verse of the Throne after every prescribed prayer to entry into Paradise with no barrier but death. > **""Whoever recites the Verse of the Throne after every prescribed prayer will be barred from entering Paradise by nothing but death.""**"
"16923","The narration adds that the same barrier-free promise applies to one who recites the chapter of Sincerity after the prayer. > **""Whoever recites: Say: He is Allah, the One, after every prayer will be barred from entering Paradise by nothing but death.""**"
"16924","Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised divine protection until the next prayer for reciting the Verse of the Throne after the prescribed prayer. > **""Whoever recites the Verse of the Throne after the prescribed prayer will remain under the protection of Allah until the next prayer.""**"
"16925","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named three deeds brought with faith that admit one through whichever gate of Paradise he wishes and marry him to whichever wide-eyed maiden he wishes, and when Abu Bakr asked whether even one suffices, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed even one. > **""Three deeds, when one brings them with faith, admit him through whichever gate of Paradise he wishes: to pardon one's killer, to repay a hidden debt, and to recite: Say: He is Allah, the One, ten times after every prescribed prayer; and even one of them suffices.""**"
"16926","Abdullah ibn Arqam reported from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, the promise of the fullest measure of reward for a threefold recitation of the closing verses of the chapter of the Ranks after every prayer. > **""Whoever says after every prayer three times: Glory belongs to your Lord, the Lord of Might, above what they ascribe; and peace be upon the messengers; and praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, will have measured out the fullest measure of reward.""**"
"16927","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Companions recognized the conclusion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayer by his utterance of the closing verses of the chapter of the Ranks. > **""Glory belongs to your Lord, the Lord of Might, above what they ascribe; and peace be upon the messengers; and praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds.""**"
"16928","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that the speaker of a coupled glorification and treasure phrase after the prayer rises forgiven. > **""Whoever says after the prayer: Glory belongs to Allah, the Tremendous, and with His praise, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, will rise with his sins forgiven.""**"
"16929","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed, he used to utter a declaration of oneness with life, death, and power, followed by a plea on divine granting and withholding. > **""There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death, and He has power over all things. O Allah, none withholds what You grant, none grants what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate avails him nothing against You.""**"
"16930","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prayer, he used to utter a declaration of oneness affirming that all goodness is in Allah Hand, followed by a plea on divine granting and withholding. > **""There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; goodness is in His Hand, and He has power over all things. O Allah, none withholds what You grant, none grants what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate avails him nothing against You.""**"
"16931","Mughira ibn Shuba, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a declaration of oneness after the prayer affirming that Allah gives life, causes death, and is the Living Who never dies, with all goodness in His Hand. > **""There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death, and He is the Living Who never dies; goodness is in His Hand, and He has power over all things.""**"
"16932","Makhul reported that he heard Muawiya, may Allah be pleased with him, say upon the minbar that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned from the prayer, he used to utter a declaration of oneness followed by a plea on divine granting and withholding. > **""There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things. O Allah, none withholds what You grant, none grants what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate avails him nothing against You.""**"
"16933","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described ten phrases said after prayers that earn ten good deeds, erase ten evil deeds, raise ten degrees, equal ten freed slaves, and guard from Satan until evening, with the same for the evening until morning. > **""There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things. Allah records ten good deeds, erases ten evil deeds, raises ten degrees, and they guard him from Satan until evening.""**"
"16934","Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness even for flight from the battlefield to one who seeks Allah forgiveness with the Living, Sustaining formula after every prayer. > **""Whoever says after every prayer: I seek the forgiveness of Allah, besides Whom there is no god, the Living, the Sustainer, and I turn to Him, will be forgiven even if he had fled from the battlefield.""**"
"16935","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray two units before the rising of dawn, utter a plea naming the Lord of Jibril, Mikail, Israfil, and Muhammad while still at his place of prayer, and then go out to the prayer. > **""O Allah, Lord of Jibril and Mikail, Lord of Israfil and Lord of Muhammad, I seek refuge in You from the Fire.""**"
"16936","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred sitting to remember Allah with magnification, praise, glorification, and oneness until sunrise over freeing two or more slaves from the children of Ismail, and preferred the same from after the afternoon prayer until sunset over freeing four. > **""For me to sit remembering Allah, magnifying Him, praising Him, glorifying Him, and declaring His oneness until the sun rises is dearer to me than freeing two slaves from the children of Ismail; and from after the afternoon prayer until the sun sets is dearer to me than freeing four.""**"
"16937","In another narration, the Prophet ﷺ preferred remembering Allah from the rising of dawn until sunrise with magnification, oneness, and glorification over freeing four from the children of Ismail, and remembering Allah from the afternoon prayer until sunset over freeing a like number. > **""For me to remember Allah from the rising of dawn until the rising of the sun is dearer to me than freeing four from the children of Ismail; and to remember Allah from the afternoon prayer until the sun disappears is dearer to me than freeing so many.""**"
"16938","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the reward of a pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage to one who prays the morning prayer in congregation, sits remembering Allah until sunrise, then stands and prays two units. > **""Whoever prays the morning prayer in congregation, then sits remembering Allah until the sun rises, then stands and prays two units, returns with the reward of a pilgrimage and a lesser pilgrimage.""**"
"16939","Abdullah ibn Amir reported that Abu Umama and Utba ibn Abd, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the promise of a complete pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage for one who prays dawn in the mosque congregation and remains until the forenoon prayer. > **""Whoever prays the dawn prayer in congregation, then remains until the forenoon prayer, earns like the reward of a pilgrim and one performing the lesser pilgrimage, complete.""**"
"16940","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not rise from his seat after the dawn prayer until prayer became permissible, and promised the rank of an accepted pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage to one who does likewise. > **""Whoever prays the dawn prayer and then remains seated until prayer becomes permissible for him attains the rank of an accepted pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage.""**"
"16941","Amra reported that she heard the Mother of the Believers, Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, say that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise that one who prays dawn, remains seated without idle worldly engagement while remembering Allah, then prays four units of the forenoon prayer, leaves his sins as on the day his mother bore him. > **""Whoever prays the dawn prayer, then remains in his seat without idle worldly engagement, remembering Allah until he prays four units of the forenoon prayer, leaves his sins as on the day his mother bore him.""**"
"16942","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred one who prays the afternoon prayer and sits conveying good until evening over one who frees eight persons from the children of Ismail. > **""Whoever prays the afternoon prayer and then sits conveying good until evening arrives is better than one who frees eight persons from the children of Ismail.""**"
"16943","In a narration of Abu Yala, the Prophet ﷺ preferred sitting with people who remember Allah from early morning until sunrise over everything upon which the sun rises. > **""For me to sit with people who remember Allah from the early morning until the sun rises is dearer to me than everything upon which the sun rises.""**"
"16944","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred sitting with rememberers of Allah after dawn until sunrise and after the afternoon prayer until sunset over freeing four from the children of Ismail at twelve thousand each. > **""For me to sit with people who remember Allah after the dawn prayer until the sun rises is dearer to me than freeing four from the children of Ismail; and after the afternoon prayer until the sun sets is dearer to me than freeing four from them.""**"
"16945","Muadh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made Paradise binding for one who prays dawn and sits remembering Allah until sunrise. > **""Whoever prays the dawn prayer and then sits remembering Allah until the sun rises earns Paradise as binding upon him.""**"
"16946","Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred witnessing dawn and sitting to remember Allah until sunrise over equipping fine horses in the path of Allah until sunrise. > **""For me to witness the dawn prayer and then sit remembering Allah until the sun rises is dearer to me than mounting fine horses in the path of Allah until the sun rises.""**"
"16947","Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred sitting from the morning prayer until sunrise over freeing four slaves from the children of Ismail, with another wording preferring the morning prayer and remembrance until sunrise over preparing horses in the path of Allah until sunrise. > **""For me to sit from the morning prayer until the sun rises is dearer to me than freeing four slaves from the children of Ismail.""**"
"16948","Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that he heard his grandfather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, make sitting to remember Allah after dawn until sunrise a veil against the Fire. > **""No servant prays the dawn prayer and then sits remembering Allah until the sun rises except that it becomes a veil for him against the Fire.""**"
"16949","Umayr ibn al-Mamum reported that he came to Madina visiting a cousin married to Hasan ibn Ali, joined him for dawn in the mosque of the Messenger ﷺ, and witnessed Ibn al-Zubayr feast invitation. Hasan turned to Umayr asking whether the sun had risen, and when Umayr thought it had, Hasan praised Allah Who raised it, then cited his father and grandfather, meaning the Prophet ﷺ, on sitting after dawn as a screen from the Fire. Hasan accepted the invitation while fasting, and when Ibn al-Zubayr mentioned a gift, Hasan cited his father and grandfather on the gift of the fasting person. When Umayr asked for repetition, Hasan cited them on the gains of frequenting the mosque. > **""Whoever prays the morning prayer and then sits remembering Allah until the sun rises, Allah sets a screen between him and the Fire. And whoever frequents the mosque attains a decisive verse, an awaited mercy, fresh knowledge, or a word that increases guidance or turns him from ruin.""**"
"16950","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while the Prophet ﷺ sat with Abu Bakr, Ibn Masud, Muadh ibn Jabal, and Nuaym ibn Salama, Burayd returned from an expedition, and Abu Bakr marveled at its speed and spoils, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ pointed to a swifter return and better spoils. > **""O Abu Bakr, shall I not guide you to what returns faster and yields better spoils? Whoever prays the morning prayer in congregation and then remembers Allah until the sun rises.""**"
"16951","Ata ibn al-Saib reported that he visited Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami after dawn while seated in the mosque and suggested his bed would be softer, but Abu Abd al-Rahman cited Ali, who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise the angels prayer of forgiveness and mercy for one who sits in his place of prayer after dawn, and likewise for one who awaits the prayer. > **""Whoever prays the dawn prayer and then sits in his place of prayer is prayed for by the angels: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy upon him.""**"
"16952","Jabir ibn Samura, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet ﷺ practice after the dawn prayer of sitting in remembrance until sunrise. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ prayed the dawn prayer, he would sit remembering Allah until the sun rose.""**"
"16953","Mudrik reported that he passed by Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, as he sat after the morning prayer, and when Mudrik asked what kept him seated, Bilal answered that he awaited the rising of the sun. > **""I am awaiting the rising of the sun.""**"
"16954","Ibrahim al-Nakhai reported from one who saw Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that Ibn Masud prayed dawn, remained seated without rising for any prayer until the call for noon, then rose and prayed four units."
"16955","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the reward of four freed slaves, ten good deeds, ten erased evils, and a shield from Satan until evening for ten declarations of oneness after dawn, with the same after sunset. > **""Whoever says after the dawn prayer ten times: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, earns like freeing four slaves and finds them a shield from Satan until evening.""**"
"16956","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm reported from the Prophet ﷺ the tenfold declaration after sunset and dawn before departing or straightening the legs, with ten good deeds per utterance, ten erasures, ten degrees, shields from harm and Satan, immunity from sin but association, and rank among the best in deeds. > **""Whoever says after the sunset and dawn prayers ten times: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; in His Hand is goodness; He gives life and causes death, and He has power over all things, earns ten good deeds for each, and no sin will overtake him except association with Allah.""**"
"16957","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, complained to the Prophet ﷺ of household labor with blistered hands from grinding and kneading. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that any provision from Allah would reach her, then guided her to a hundredfold bedtime formula better than a servant and to tenfold declarations after dawn and sunset carrying tenfold good deeds, erasures, emancipation-like reward, protection from sin but association, and guardianship from Satan and evil between morning and evening. > **""When you retire to bed, glorify Allah thirty-three times, magnify Him thirty-three times, and praise Him thirty-four times, for that is better for you than a servant. And after the dawn and sunset prayers say ten times: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death; in His Hand is goodness, and He has power over all things.""**"
"16958","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised ten good deeds per utterance, ten erasures, ten degrees, daily shielding from harm, guarding from Satan, emancipation-like reward at twelve thousand per slave, and immunity from sin but association, for ten declarations with legs still folded before speech after dawn, with the like after sunset. > **""Whoever says after the dawn prayer, with his legs still folded and before speaking, ten times: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death; in His Hand is goodness, and He has power over all things, earns ten good deeds for each and finds them a guard against Satan.""**"
"16959","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ranked one who says a declaration of oneness with life, death, goodness, and power a hundred times after the morning prayer before moving his legs among the best of the earth in deeds. > **""Whoever says after the morning prayer one hundred times, before straightening his legs: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death; in His Hand is goodness, and He has power over all things, becomes among the best of the people of the earth in deeds.""**"
"16960","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised seven grants for ten declarations when finishing dawn, with the like at sunset for the night. > **""Whoever says when finishing the morning prayer ten times: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; in His Hand is goodness, and He has power over all things, earns ten good deeds, ten degrees, protection from Satan, and no sin overtakes him except association with Allah.""**"
"16961","Zumayl al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after dawn, with legs still folded, said a glorification with repentance seventy times, declared its multiplication to seven hundred, warned of exceeding that in daily sins, then faced the people. > **""Glory belongs to Allah and with His praise; I seek the forgiveness of Allah, for He is the Oft-Returning. Seventy multiplied by seven hundred; there is no good in one whose sins in a single day exceed seven hundred.""**"
"16962","Asma bint Wathila ibn al-Asqa, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that her father after dawn sat facing the prayer direction in silence until sunrise, and when she questioned him after he declined speech over a need, he cited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a hundred recitations of the chapter of Sincerity before speech. > **""Whoever prays the dawn prayer and then recites: Say: He is Allah, the One, one hundred times before speaking is forgiven a year's sin with every recitation.""**"
"16963","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the gates of heaven open and supplication is answered when prayer is established, and warned that angels, Paradise, and the wide-eyed maidens reproach one who leaves without asking for refuge, Paradise, and marriage to them. > **""O Allah, save me from the Fire, admit me into Paradise, and marry me to the wide-eyed maidens.""**"
"16964","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he brought the Prophet ﷺ ablution water, and after the Prophet ﷺ performed ablution and prayed, he supplicated for his religion, self, and provision. > **""O Allah, set right for me my religion, widen for me my self, and bless for me my provision.""**"
"16965","Yahya ibn Hassan al-Filastini reported from a man of the Banu Kinana that he prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ in the year of the Conquest and heard him supplicate against disgrace on the Day of Resurrection. > **""O Allah, do not disgrace me on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16966","Zadhan reported from an Ansari Companion of the Prophet ﷺ that he heard the Prophet ﷺ repeat a plea for forgiveness and repentance a hundred times in his prayer. > **""O Allah, forgive me and turn to me, for You are the Oft-Returning, the Forgiving.""**"
"16967","Ubayd ibn al-Qaqa reported that a man observed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in prayer saying a supplication for forgiveness, a spacious home, and blessed provision. > **""O Allah, forgive my sin, widen for me my home, and bless for me in what You have provided me.""**"
"16968","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she missed the Prophet ﷺ from his resting place, felt for him with her hand in the dark, and found him prostrate, supplicating for his soul. > **""My Lord, grant my soul its piety, purify it, for You are the best to purify it; You are its Guardian and its Master.""**"
"16969","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a plea naming the Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Israfil at the end of every prayer. > **""O Allah, Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Israfil, grant me refuge from the heat of the Fire and the punishment of the grave.""**"
"16970","Abu al-Malih ibn Usama reported from his father that the Prophet ﷺ prayed a prayer and he heard him say a plea naming the Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Muhammad. > **""Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Muhammad, save me from the Fire.""**"
"16971","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Prophet ﷺ prayed and finished his prayer, he would wipe his head with his right hand and invoke the Name of Allah, the One besides Whom there is no god, the Most Merciful, the Mercy-Giving, asking removal of anxiety and grief. > **""In the Name of Allah, besides Whom there is no god, the Most Merciful, the Mercy-Giving; O Allah, remove from me anxiety and grief.""**"
"16972","In another narration, the Prophet ﷺ would wipe his forehead with his right hand and utter a plea for the removal of anxiety and grief. > **""O Allah, remove from me anxiety and grief.""**"
"16973","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never led them in a prescribed prayer without saying, upon turning to face them, a fivefold plea for refuge from disgracing deeds, ruinous companions, distracting hopes, forgetful poverty, and transgressing wealth. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from every deed that disgraces me, from a companion who destroys me, from every hope that distracts me, from every poverty that makes me forget, and from every wealth that makes me transgress.""**"
"16974","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that his station stood facing between the shoulders of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and whenever the Prophet ﷺ gave the greeting of peace, he would supplicate for the best of life, the sealing of deeds, and the best of days. > **""O Allah, make the best of my life its end; O Allah, make the seal of my deeds Your pleasure; O Allah, make the best of my days the day I meet You.""**"
"16975","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he never prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ without hearing him say, upon finishing, a plea for the forgiveness of all faults and sins, for revival, mending, and guidance to righteous deeds and character that none guides to or turns away from but Allah. > **""O Allah, forgive my faults and all my sins; O Allah, revive me, mend me, and guide me to righteous deeds and character, for none guides to their good nor turns away their evil but You.""**"
"16976","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a plea for wholesome provision, beneficial knowledge, and accepted deeds after the dawn prayer. > **""O Allah, I ask You for wholesome provision, beneficial knowledge, and accepted deeds.""**"
"16977","Abu Barza al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the dawn prayer, he would raise his voice so his Companions heard him repeat three times a plea for his religion and worldly life, three times a plea of refuge in divine pleasure from wrath, and a closing declaration that none withholds or grants but Allah and no fortune avails against Him. > **""O Allah, set right for me my religion which You made my protection. O Allah, set right for me my worldly life in which You placed my livelihood. O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and I seek refuge in You from You. O Allah, none withholds what You grant, none grants what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate avails him nothing against You.""**"
"16978","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the dawn prayer, he would raise his voice so his Companions heard him repeat three times pleas for his religion, worldly life, and hereafter, three times refuge in pleasure from wrath, three times refuge in pardon from punishment, and a closing declaration that none withholds or grants but Allah. > **""O Allah, set right for me my religion which You made my protection. O Allah, set right for me my worldly life in which You placed my livelihood. O Allah, set right for me my hereafter to which You made my return. O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath. O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pardon from Your punishment. O Allah, none withholds what You grant, none grants what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate avails him nothing against You.""**"
"16979","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that Qabisa ibn al-Mukhariq al-Hilali came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, greeted him, and was greeted and welcomed in return. When the Prophet ﷺ asked what had brought him, O Qabisa, he told of his advanced age, thin skin, weakened strength, burden upon his family, and inability for deeds he once did, asking for brief beneficial words. The Prophet ﷺ told him to say three times after the morning prayer a glorification with praise and the treasure phrase, promising safety by Allah leave from blindness, leprosy, and vitiligo, and to add a fourfold plea for guidance, bounty, mercy, and blessings. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept repeating the phrases while Qabisa counted them upon his fingers. > **""O Qabisa, say three times after the morning prayer: Glory belongs to Allah and with His praise; glory belongs to Allah, the Tremendous, and with His praise; and there is no power nor strength except through Allah. And say: O Allah, guide me from Yourself, pour upon me from Your bounty, spread upon me from Your mercy, and send down upon me from Your blessings.""**"
"16980","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a man may stand in prayer and supplicate a supplication by which he is forgiven, along with the Muslims behind him. > **""A man may stand in prayer and supplicate a supplication by which he is forgiven, along with the Muslims behind him.""**"
"16981","Abu Umama reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever supplicates after every prescribed prayer with a plea asking Allah to grant Muhammad the Elevated Station and high rank among the chosen and the near ones secures his intercession on the Day of Resurrection. > **""O Allah, grant Muhammad the Elevated Station, place his love among the chosen, his rank among the worlds, and his abode among the near ones.""**"
"16982","Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that whenever he drew near the Prophet ﷺ in a prescribed or voluntary prayer, he heard him supplicate with the same phrases for the forgiveness of all sins and faults, for revival, mending, and guidance to righteous deeds and character, without adding to them or lessening from them. > **""O Allah, forgive me my sins and all my faults; O Allah, revive me, mend me, and guide me to righteous deeds and character, for none guides to their good nor turns away their evil but You.""**"
"16983","Abdullah ibn Busr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever opens the start of his day with good and seals it with good will have Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, command His angels not to record against him the sins committed between the two. > **""Whoever opens the start of his day with good and seals it with good, Allah will say to His angels: Do not record against him the sins between the two.""**"
"16984","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever says in the morning ten times a declaration of oneness affirming that Allah gives life and causes death and has power over all things earns ten good deeds per utterance, ten erasures, ten degrees, the equal of ten freed slaves, and protection from the start of day to its end, with no deed overpowering them; and whoever says them in the evening receives the like. > **""Whoever says in the morning ten times: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death, and He has power over all things, Allah records ten good deeds per utterance, erases ten evil deeds, raises ten degrees, and they guard him from the start of day to its end.""**"
"16985","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived at Madina and stayed in his house, the Prophet ﷺ offered to teach him, and when Abu Ayyub eagerly accepted, the Prophet ﷺ taught him a morning declaration of oneness carrying ten good deeds, ten erasures, the equal of ten freed slaves before Allah, and protection from Satan until evening, with the like for the evening. > **""O Abu Ayyub, shall I not teach you? No servant says in the morning: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner, except that Allah records ten good deeds, erases ten evil deeds, and they equal ten freed slaves before Allah, and he is shielded from Satan until evening; and whoever says them in the evening receives the like.""**"
"16986","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever says a declaration of oneness ten times in the morning earns a hundred good deeds, a hundred erasures, the equal of a freed slave, and protection that day until evening, with the like for the evening. > **""Whoever says ten times in the morning: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things, earns a hundred good deeds, a hundred erasures, the equal of a freed slave, and protection that day until evening.""**"
"16987","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever says morning and evening a declaration of oneness affirming that Allah gives life, causes death, is Living without death, holds all goodness, and has power over all things will have his sins forgiven even if like the foam of the sea. > **""Whoever says in the day, morning and evening: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death, and He is Living without death; in His Hand is goodness, and He has power over all things, will have his sins forgiven even if like the foam of the sea.""**"
"16988","Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught him a supplication and commanded him to enjoin it upon his household every day, a covenant beginning with devoted response to Allah, submission of all speech, oaths, prayers, and curses to His will, allegiance in this world and the next, requests for contentment, post-death comfort, the delight of beholding His Face, longing for His meeting without harmful trial, refuge from wronging or being wronged, testimony of divine oneness and Muhammad servitude and messengership, affirmation of the promise, meeting, Paradise, and the undoubted Hour with resurrection, confession of helplessness without mercy, and a plea for total forgiveness and repentance. > **""Whoever says in the morning: At Your service, O Allah, at Your service; all good is in Your Hands, from You, through You, and to You. Whatever I utter, vow, or swear, Your will precedes it. O Allah, Originator of the heavens and earth, Knower of the unseen and seen, I pledge to You in this worldly life that there is no god but You alone without partner, and that Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger; if You entrust me to myself You entrust me to ruin, so forgive all my sins, for none forgives sins but You, and turn to me, for You are the Oft-Returning, the Mercy-Giving.""**"
"16989","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged that none of them abandon earning a thousand good deeds for Allah each day by saying a hundred times in the morning an invocation beginning with the Name of Allah and glorification with praise, which yields a thousand good deeds, with sins unlikely to match it and remaining good in surplus. > **""Let none of you abandon earning a thousand good deeds for Allah each day: say in the morning a hundred times: In the Name of Allah; glory belongs to Allah and with His praise, for that is a thousand good deeds.""**"
"16990","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever says a thousand times in the morning a glorification with praise has bought himself free from Allah and ends his day as one freed by Allah. > **""Whoever says in the morning a thousand times: Glory belongs to Allah and with His praise, has bought himself free from Allah, and ends his day as one freed by Allah.""**"
"16991","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever says a hundred times in the morning a glorification with praise has bought himself free from Allah and ends his day as one freed by Allah. > **""Whoever says in the morning a hundred times: Glory belongs to Allah and with His praise, has bought himself free from Allah, and ends his day as one freed by Allah.""**"
"16992","Abu Umama al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught a threefold morning and evening covenant of praise, oneness, servitude, and repentance, promising Paradise to one who dies that day or night, and swore an oath upon it unlike his oaths upon other matters. > **""Whoever says three times in the morning: O Allah, to You belongs praise; there is no god but You; You are my Lord and I am Your servant; I enter morning upon Your covenant and promise as I am able; I turn to You from the evil of my deeds and seek Your forgiveness for sins none forgives but You, and dies that day, enters Paradise; and whoever says them in the evening and dies that night, enters Paradise.""**"
"16993","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a plea in the morning affirming life, death, and return through Allah. > **""O Allah, by You we enter morning, by You we enter evening, by You we live and die, and to You is the return.""**"
"16994","Abu Hurayra reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to say in the morning a declaration that dominion belongs to Allah with praise, oneness, and return to resurrection, and in the evening the like with return to the final destination. > **""We enter morning and dominion belongs to Allah; praise belongs to Allah without partner; there is no god but Him, and to Him is the resurrection. And we enter evening and dominion belongs to Allah; praise belongs to Allah without partner; there is no god but Him, and to Him is the destination.""**"
"16995","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she used to hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, when evening reached him in her house, a declaration of evening dominion with praise, of power, ability, and sovereignty in the heavens and earth with all things belonging to Allah, followed by the plea of entering morning and evening through Allah with life, death, and resurrection. > **""We enter evening and dominion belongs to Allah; praise belongs to Allah; power, ability, and sovereignty are in the heavens and earth, and all things belong to Allah, Lord of the worlds. O Allah, by You we enter morning, by You we enter evening, by You we live and die, and to You is the resurrection.""**"
"16996","Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say morning and evening a declaration of dominion with oneness, asking the good of the day and what follows it, with refuge from its evil, from the evil of old age, and from the punishment of the Fire. > **""We enter morning and dominion belongs to Allah; there is no god but Allah alone, without partner. O Allah, we ask You the good of this day and the good after it, and we seek refuge in You from the evil of this day and the evil after it. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of old age, and I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the Fire.""**"
"16997","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say in the morning a declaration that dominion, pride, greatness, creation, night, day, and all that rests therein belong to Allah alone without partner, followed by a plea for a successful start, middle, and end of the day and the good of this world and the next. > **""I enter morning and dominion, pride, greatness, creation, night, day, and all that rests therein belong to Allah alone, without partner. O Allah, make the start of this day, its middle, and its end successful; I ask You the good of this world and the next, O Most Merciful of the merciful.""**"
"16998","Abu Saeed, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Prophet ﷺ entered morning and the sun rose, he would utter a testimony witnessed to by Allah, His angels, the learned, and those who testify not, asking it be recorded in place of the latters, followed by pleas for peace, answered supplication, enrichment, and the setting right of religion, worldly life, and hereafter. > **""O Allah, I enter morning bearing witness to what You witnessed of Yourself, with Your angels, the learned, and those who did not witness as You witnessed; so record my testimony in place of theirs. O Allah, You are Peace, from You comes peace, and to You peace returns; O Possessor of Majesty and Honor, answer our supplication, grant our desire, enrich us beyond Your creation, set right my religion which guards my affair, my worldly life of my livelihood, and my hereafter of my return.""**"
"16999","Abdullah ibn al-Qasim reported that a neighboring woman of the Prophet ﷺ told him she used to hear the Prophet ﷺ say at the rising of dawn a plea for refuge from the punishment and trial of the grave. Abu Isa asked Abdullah whether a man might combine the two, and Abdullah answered with what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave and from the trial of the grave.""**"
"17000","Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the interpretation of the verse concerning the keys of the heavens and earth. The Prophet ﷺ told him none had asked before him, then interpreted it as a formula of oneness, magnification, glorification with praise, seeking forgiveness, and the treasure phrase with the First, Last, Manifest, Hidden, goodness in His Hand, life, death, and power. Whoever says it ten times in the morning is granted ten traits: protection from Iblis and his hosts, a weighty measure of reward, a raised degree in Paradise, marriage to wide-eyed maidens, the presence of twelve thousand angels, reward like reading the revealed Books, and, O Uthman, like accepted pilgrimage and lesser pilgrimage, with the seal of martyrs if he dies that day. > **""Its interpretation is: There is no god but Allah; Allah is Greatest; glory belongs to Allah and with His praise; I seek Allah forgiveness; and there is no power nor strength except through Allah, the First, the Last, the Manifest, the Hidden; in His Hand is goodness; He gives life and causes death, and He has power over all things. Whoever says it ten times in the morning is granted ten traits, and if he dies that day he is sealed with the seal of martyrs.""**"
"17001","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate morning and evening for sudden good and refuge from sudden evil, for the servant knows not what will strike him. > **""O Allah, I ask You for sudden good, and I seek refuge in You from sudden evil, for the servant knows not what will strike him morning or evening.""**"
"17002","Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to teach them to say in the morning a declaration of the primordial faith of submission, the word of sincerity, the practice of Muhammad ﷺ, and the creed of Ibrahim, upright and submitted, never among the associators, and to say the like in the evening. > **""We enter morning upon the primordial faith of submission, the word of sincerity, the practice of our Prophet Muhammad, and the creed of our father Ibrahim, upright and submitted, never among the associators.""**"
"17003","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to say morning and evening a declaration of the primordial faith, the word of sincerity, the religion of Muhammad ﷺ, and the creed of Ibrahim, upright and submitted, never among the associators. > **""We enter morning upon the primordial faith of submission, upon the word of sincerity, upon the religion of our Prophet Muhammad, and upon the creed of our father Ibrahim, upright and submitted, never among the associators.""**"
"17004","Abu Sallam reported that a man passed through the mosque of Hims and the people said he had served the Prophet ﷺ, so Abu Sallam asked him for a narration heard directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The man, named by Ahmad as Sabiq, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that no Muslim servant says three times morning and evening a declaration of contentment with Allah as Lord, submission as religion, and Muhammad ﷺ as Prophet without Allah making it binding upon Himself to content him on the Day of Resurrection. > **""No Muslim servant says three times morning and evening: I am content with Allah as Lord, with submission as religion, and with Muhammad as Prophet, without Allah making it binding upon Himself to content him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17005","Munaydhir, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who resided in Africa, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise that whoever says in the morning a declaration of contentment with Allah, submission, and Muhammad as Prophet will have him as guarantor to take his hand until he admits him to Paradise. > **""Whoever says in the morning: I am content with Allah as Lord, with submission as religion, and with Muhammad as Prophet, I am his guarantor to take his hand until I admit him to Paradise.""**"
"17006","Aban al-Muharibi, of the delegation that came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that no Muslim servant says morning and evening a praise of Allah with disavowal of association and testimony of oneness without his sins being forgiven until evening, or until morning if said in the evening. > **""No Muslim servant says morning and evening: Praise belongs to Allah, I associate nothing with Him, and I testify there is no god but Allah, without his sins being forgiven until evening; and if he says them in the evening, until morning.""**"
"17007","Hakam ibn Hayyan al-Muharibi, from Aban al-Muharibi of the Abd al-Qays delegation to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that no Muslim servant says in the morning a praise of his Lord with disavowal of association and testimony of oneness without his sins being forgiven through the day until evening, and if said in the evening, through the night until morning. > **""No Muslim servant says in the morning: Praise belongs to Allah my Lord, I associate nothing with Him, and I testify there is no god but Allah, without his sins being forgiven through the day until evening.""**"
"17008","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, asking what prevents her from hearing his counsel: to say morning and evening an invocation of the Ever-Living, the Sustainer, seeking aid by His mercy, the setting right of all affairs, and entrustment not to oneself for a blink. > **""What prevents you from hearing what I counsel you: say morning and evening: O Ever-Living, O Sustainer, by Your mercy I seek aid; set right for me all my affairs, and entrust me not to myself for the blink of an eye.""**"
"17009","Abu Umama al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered morning and evening, he supplicated with a glorification of Allah as most worthy of remembrance, worship, seeking, ownership, asking, and giving, the King without partner, the Unique Who perishes not while all perishes but His Face, obeyed only by permission and disobeyed only with knowledge, thankful to the obedient and forgiving to the disobedient, nearest witness and closest guardian, lawful and unlawful by His decree, and a plea by the light of His Face and every right to be accepted that morning or evening and shielded from the Fire by His power. > **""O Allah, You are most worthy of remembrance, most worthy of worship, most aiding of the sought, most merciful of owners, most generous of the asked, and most ample of givers. You are the King without partner, the Unique Who perishes not; all things perish but Your Face. I ask You by the light of Your Face by which the heavens and earth shone to accept me this morning and to shield me from the Fire by Your power.""**"
"17010","Muadh ibn Anas reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he disclosed why Allah named Ibrahim His close friend, the one who fulfilled: because every morning and evening he recited the glorification of the opening verses of the chapter of the Romans, through to their end. > **""Shall I not inform you why Allah named Ibrahim His close friend, the one who fulfilled? Because every morning and evening he said: Glory belongs to Allah when you enter evening and when you enter morning.""**"
"17011","Umm Salama reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever says in the morning a praise of Allah before Whose greatness all things humble has good deeds recorded for him. > **""Whoever says in the morning: Praise belongs to Allah before Whose greatness all things humble, has good deeds recorded for him.""**"
"17012","Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he kept a heap of dates that kept diminishing, so he guarded it one night and met a creature like an adolescent youth. He greeted him, received the greeting, and asked whether he was jinn or man; the creature said jinn. When Ubayy took his hand, it was a dog hand with dog hair, and the creature confirmed this is the form of the jinn, boasting none among them stronger than he. Asked what brought him, he said word had reached them of Ubayy love of charity, so they came for his food. Asked what saves from them, he named the verse of the Throne in the chapter of the Cow: whoever says it in the evening is shielded from them until morning, and whoever says it in the morning is shielded until evening. In the morning Ubayy told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who affirmed the wicked one had spoken truth. > **""The wicked one spoke truth. Whoever says the verse of the Throne in the evening is shielded from them until morning, and whoever says it in the morning is shielded until evening.""**"
"17013","Shabi reported that Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that whoever recites ten verses of the chapter of the Cow in a house, four from its start, the verse of the Throne, two after it, and its closing verses, no Satan enters that house that night until morning. > **""Whoever recites ten verses of the chapter of the Cow in a house, no Satan enters that house that night until morning.""**"
"17014","Abu Wail reported that he asked Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, one day after the dawn prayer for admission, and Ibn Masud bade them enter. When they preferred to wait briefly lest the household need something, Ibn Masud engaged in glorification, remarking on their assumption of his heedlessness, then asked his slavegirl thrice whether the sun had risen. When she twice said no and the third time said yes, he praised Allah Who granted them the day, overlooked their stumbles, and did not punish them with the Fire. > **""Praise belongs to Allah Who granted us this day, overlooked our stumbles therein, and did not punish us with the Fire.""**"
"17015","Hasan reported that Samura ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, offered him a narration heard repeatedly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar: whoever says morning and evening a sixfold declaration that Allah created, guides, feeds, gives drink, causes death, and gives life will not ask Allah anything without being granted it. When Hasan conveyed it to Abdullah ibn Salam, he responded with devotion to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that these were words Allah had granted Musa, peace be upon him, who supplicated with them seven times daily and was never refused. > **""Whoever says morning and evening: O Allah, You created me, You guide me, You feed me, You give me drink, You cause me to die, and You give me life, will not ask Allah anything without being granted it.""**"
"17016","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever says in the morning a testimony calling Allah, the Throne bearers, angels, and all creation to witness His sole oneness and Muhammad servitude and messengership will have Allah forgive the sins he commits that day, and likewise at night. > **""Whoever says in the morning: O Allah, I call You to witness, with the bearers of Your Throne, Your angels, and all Your creation, that You alone have no partner and that Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger, Allah forgives the sins he commits that day; and whoever says them in the evening, Allah forgives what befalls him that night.""**"
"17017","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered evening, he would declare the evening dominion of Allah, the One, the Overwhelming, with praise for the departure of day, arrival of night, and wellness, asking pardon for the day evils, acceptance and manifold increase for its good, and the success of every need that night without increase in worldliness or decrease in the hereafter; and he said the like in the morning. > **""We enter evening and dominion belongs to Allah, the One, the Overwhelming; praise belongs to Allah Who took the day and brought the night, while we are in wellness. O Allah, whatever evil I did therein, pardon it; whatever good I did therein, accept and multiply it manifold. O Allah, fulfill every need of mine this night; increase me not in my worldliness nor decrease me in my hereafter.""**"
"17018","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught an evening declaration of dominion with praise and refuge in Allah, Who holds the sky from falling upon the earth but by His leave, from the evil of all He created and spread; whoever says them is shielded from every sorcerer, soothsayer, Satan, and envier. > **""We enter evening and dominion belongs to Allah; praise belongs to Allah. I seek refuge in Allah, Who holds the sky from falling upon the earth but by His leave, from the evil of all He created and spread; whoever says them is shielded from every sorcerer, soothsayer, Satan, and envier.""**"
"17019","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered morning, he would declare thrice a testimony calling his Lord, angels, prophets, messengers, and all creation to witness his affirmation of Allah sole oneness, Muhammad servitude and messengership, faith, and trust. > **""I enter morning, O Lord, calling You, Your angels, prophets, messengers, and all Your creation to witness my testimony: I testify You are Allah, there is no god but You alone without partner, Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger, I believe in You and trust in You.""**"
"17020","Ibn Abbas reported, raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that whoever says thrice in the morning a covenant of praise, oneness, servitude, and repentance will have Allah forbid him to the Fire that day. > **""Whoever says thrice in the morning: O Allah, to You belongs praise; there is no god but You alone without partner; You are my Lord and I am Your servant; I enter morning upon Your covenant and promise as I am able; I turn to You from the evil of my deeds and seek Your forgiveness for my sins, which none forgives but You, Allah forbids him to the Fire.""**"
"17021","Abdullah ibn Busr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever opens the start of his day with good and seals it with good will have Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, say to His angels not to record against him the sins between the two. > **""Whoever opens the start of his day with good and seals it with good, Allah will say to His angels: Do not record against him the sins between the two.""**"
"17022","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever invokes blessings upon him ten times in the morning and ten times in the evening will have his intercession reach him on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever invokes blessings upon me ten times in the morning and ten times in the evening will have my intercession reach him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17023","Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever says morning and evening a refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created will suffer no harm. > **""Whoever says morning and evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, will suffer no harm.""**"
"17024","In a narration with him, whoever says in the evening a refuge in all the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created will suffer no harm. > **""Whoever says in the evening: I seek refuge in all the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, will suffer no harm.""**"
"17025","In another narration with him, whoever says when the sun disappears a refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created will suffer no harm that night. > **""Whoever says when the sun disappears: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, will suffer no harm that night.""**"
"17026","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when it was said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a man had not slept the night, and asked why, the answer came that a scorpion had stung him, the Prophet ﷺ declared that had he said upon retiring to bed a refuge in the perfect words of Allah, it would not have harmed him. > **""Had he said upon retiring to his bed: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, it would not have harmed him.""**"
"17027","Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that no man retires to bed reciting a chapter of the Book of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, without Allah dispatching an angel to guard him from all harm until he rises whenever he rises. > **""No man retires to his bed reciting a chapter of the Book of Allah without Allah dispatching an angel to guard him from all harm until he rises.""**"
"17028","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a man retires to bed, an angel and a Satan hasten to him, the angel urging him to seal with good and the Satan to seal with evil; if he remembers Allah then sleeps, the angel spends the night guarding him. Upon waking, the angel urges him to open with good and the Satan with evil; if he praises Allah Who returned his soul without taking it in sleep, Who holds the heavens and earth from ceasing, and Who holds the sky from falling but by His leave, then should he fall from his bed and die, he enters Paradise. > **""When a man retires to his bed, an angel and a Satan hasten to him. If he remembers Allah then sleeps, the angel guards him; and if upon waking he says: Praise belongs to Allah Who returned my soul without taking it in my sleep, Who holds the heavens and earth from ceasing, and Who holds the sky from falling but by His leave, then falls from his bed and dies, he enters Paradise.""**"
"17029","Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would have his bed spread, face the prayer direction, and upon retiring pillow his right palm, then whisper words they could not discern; at the end he would raise his voice with a plea to the Lord of the seven heavens, the Tremendous Throne, the God of all things, Revealer of the Torah, Gospel, and Criterion, Splitter of grain and seed, for refuge from the evil of all things held by the forelock, affirming Allah as First, Last, Manifest, and Hidden, asking settlement of debt and enrichment from poverty. > **""O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens, Lord of the Tremendous Throne, God of all things, Revealer of the Torah, Gospel, and Criterion, Splitter of grain and seed, I seek refuge in You from the evil of all things You hold by the forelock. O Allah, You are the First with nothing before You, the Last with nothing after You, the Manifest with nothing above You, the Hidden with nothing beneath You; settle our debt and enrich us from poverty.""**"
"17030","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever places his side upon the bed reciting the Opener of the Book and the chapter of Sincerity is secured from all things but death. > **""When you place your side upon the bed and recite the Opener of the Book and: Say: He is Allah, the One, you are secured from all things but death.""**"
"17031","Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ that he never came to his bed without reciting the chapter of the Disbelievers through to its end. > **""The Prophet ﷺ never came to his bed without reciting: Say: O disbelievers, through to its end.""**"
"17032","Abbad ibn Akhdar, or Ahmar, reported that when the Prophet ﷺ took his resting place, he would recite the chapter of the Disbelievers through to its end. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ took his resting place, he would recite: Say: O disbelievers, through to its end.""**"
"17033","Jabala ibn Haritha, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ told him to recite the chapter of the Disbelievers through to its end upon retiring, for it is a disavowal of association. > **""When you retire to your bed, recite: Say: O disbelievers, through to its end, for it is a disavowal of association.""**"
"17034","Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded reciting the chapter of the Disbelievers upon taking the resting place, and that the Prophet ﷺ himself, upon taking his resting place, would recite it through to its end. > **""When you take your resting place, recite: Say: O disbelievers. And the Prophet ﷺ himself, upon taking his resting place, would recite it through to its end.""**"
"17035","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered a word recited at sleep that saves from association with Allah. > **""Shall I not guide you to a word that saves you from association with Allah? Recite: Say: O disbelievers, at your sleep.""**"
"17036","Abu Malik al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the son of Adam sleeps, the angel tells the Satan to hand over his scroll; whatever good the angel finds therein, he erases ten evils from the Satan scroll thereby and records them as good deeds. Whoever among them intends sleep should magnify Allah thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-four times, and glorify Him thirty-three times, making one hundred. > **""When the son of Adam sleeps, the angel takes his scroll, and for every good deed therein erases ten evils and records them as good. So when one of you intends sleep, let him magnify Allah thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-four times, and glorify Him thirty-three times, making one hundred.""**"
"17037","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining of household labor, telling him her hands had been blistered by the millstone, grinding at one time and kneading at another. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that any provision from Allah would reach her, then guided her to a hundredfold bedtime formula better than a servant. > **""If Allah grants you anything it will reach you, but I guide you to better: when you retire to your bed, glorify Allah thirty-three times, magnify Him thirty-three times, and praise Him thirty-four times, making one hundred, for that is better for you than a servant.""**"
"17038","Abdullah ibn Amr reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he commanded Fatima and Ali, peace be upon them, to glorification, praise, and magnification upon taking their resting places, though Ata could not recall which of the two phrases completes the hundred at thirty-four. > **""The Prophet ﷺ commanded Fatima and Ali, upon taking their resting places, to glorification, praise, and magnification.""**"
"17039","Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli reported that Abdullah ibn Amr brought out a paper to them, saying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach them a testimony of the Originator, Knower of unseen and seen, Lord and God of all, with witness of oneness, Muhammad servitude and messengership, angels bearing witness, and refuge from Satan, his association, harming oneself, or bringing harm upon a Muslim. Abu Abd al-Rahman added that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught it to Abdullah ibn Amr to say when intending sleep. > **""O Allah, Originator of the heavens and earth, Knower of the unseen and seen, Lord and God of all, I testify there is no god but You alone without partner, Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger, and the angels bear witness. I seek refuge in You from Satan and his association, and from bringing evil upon myself or upon a Muslim.""**"
"17040","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say, when intending sleep, a testimony of the Originator, Knower of unseen and seen, Lord and God of all, with witness of oneness, Muhammad servitude and messengership, angels bearing witness, and refuge from Satan, his association, and bringing evil upon oneself or a Muslim. Abu Abd al-Rahman added that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught it to Abdullah ibn Amr to say when intending sleep. > **""O Allah, Originator of the heavens and earth, Knower of the unseen and seen, Lord and God of all, I testify there is no god but You alone without partner, Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger, and the angels bear witness. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from Satan and his association, and from bringing evil upon myself or upon a Muslim.""**"
"17041","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say, when intending sleep, a testimony of the Originator, Knower of unseen and seen, Lord and God of all, with witness of oneness, Muhammad servitude and messengership, angels bearing witness, and refuge from Satan, his association, and committing sin against oneself or returning it upon a Muslim. > **""O Allah, Originator of the heavens and earth, Knower of the unseen and seen, Lord and God of all, I testify there is no god but You alone without partner, Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger, and the angels bear witness. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from Satan and his association, and from committing sin against myself or returning it upon a Muslim.""**"
"17042","In a narration from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, he offered to teach Abdullah ibn Yazid words the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, for when intending sleep, reciting the like of the testimony above. > **""Shall I not teach you words the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach Abu Bakr for when intending sleep?""**"
"17043","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ asked a man what he says when he retires to his bed, and the man replied that he says: in Thy Name I lay my side, so forgive my sin, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ affirmed him and invoked success for him. > **""You are right, may Allāh grant you success.""**"
"17044","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ asked a man what he says at his sleep, and the man replied that he says: in Thy Name, O Allāh, I lay my side, so forgive me, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for him. > **""May Allāh forgive you.""**"
"17045","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Prophet ﷺ lay down to sleep he would say: in Thy Name, my Lord, so forgive my sin. > **""In Thy Name, my Lord, so forgive my sin.""**"
"17046","On the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked his Companions what they say at sleep until he came to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allāh be pleased with him, who said: Thou created this soul, to Thee belong its life and death; if Thou take it, grant it health and pardon it, and if Thou return it, guard it and guide it, at which the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ marvelled at his words. > **""Thou created this soul; to Thee belong its life and its death. If Thou take it, grant it health and pardon it, and if Thou return it, guard it and guide it.""**"
"17047","On the authority of Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked Ḥamzah, may Allāh be pleased with him, what he says when he retires to his bed and he answered with such-and-such, then asked ʿAlī, may Allāh honour his face, who likewise answered, and then instructed that when one retires one should praise Allāh Who favoured him, Lord and God of all things, and seek refuge from the Fire. > **""When you retire to your bed, say: all praise belongs to Allāh Who favoured me and bestowed; all praise belongs to Allāh, Lord of the worlds, Lord of all things and God of all things. I seek refuge in Thee from the Fire.""**"
"17048","On the authority of al-Walīd ibn al-Walīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, who complained to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ of loneliness, he ﷺ instructed him when he takes his bed to seek refuge in Allāh's perfect words from His anger and punishment, from the evil of His servants, and from the whisperings of devils and their presence, for then nothing shall harm him and they shall likely not draw near him. > **""I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from His anger, His punishment, the evil of His servants, from the whisperings of the devils and lest they attend me; for it shall not harm you, and most likely they shall not come near you.""**"
"17049","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Prophet ﷺ wished to sleep he would say: O Allāh, shield me from Thy punishment on the Day Thou wilt resurrect Thy servants. > **""O Allāh, shield me from Thy punishment on the Day Thou wilt resurrect Thy servants.""**"
"17050","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ retired to his bed he would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from attachment to evil and from sleeping hungry. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from attachment to evil and from sleeping hungry.""**"
"17051","On the authority of Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever says when he retires to his bed: all praise to Allāh Who is Exalted and Irresistible, Hidden yet Aware, Sovereign and Ordaining; all praise to Allāh Who gives life and death and has power over all things, shall emerge from his sins as on the day his mother bore him. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who is Exalted and Irresistible, Hidden yet Aware, Sovereign and Ordaining. All praise belongs to Allāh Who gives life and death, and He has power over all things.""**"
"17052","On the authority of Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that each of you when he wishes to sleep should say: I believe in Allāh and disbelieve in ṭāghūt; Allāh's promise is true and the Messengers spoke truthfully; O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the visitations of this night except a visitor that comes with good. > **""I believe in Allāh and disbelieve in ṭāghūt. Allāh's promise is true and the Messengers spoke truthfully. O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the visitations of this night except a visitor that comes with good.""**"
"17053","On the authority of Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, from ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, who spent a night with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and heard him, when he finished his prayer and took his bed, say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thy pardon from Thy punishment, in Thy pleasure from Thy wrath, and in Thee from Thee; I cannot praise Thee though I strive, but Thou art as Thou praised Thyself. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thy pardon from Thy punishment, in Thy pleasure from Thy wrath, and in Thee from Thee. O Allāh, I cannot praise Thee though I strive, but Thou art as Thou praised Thyself.""**"
"17054","On the authority of Abū Isḥāq al-Hamdānī, from his father, to whom ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, wrote that the Prophet ﷺ commanded him, when he takes his bed, to seek refuge in the Noble Face of Allāh and His perfect words from the evil of all under His grasp, Who removes debt and sin, Whose army is never defeated, Whose promise never fails, and before Whom wealth avails nothing, glory and praise to Him; Abū Isḥāq mentioned it to Abū Maysarah who narrated the like from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, except he said: from the evil of what Thou seizest by its forelock. > **""When you take your bed, say: I seek refuge in the Noble Face of Allāh and His perfect words from the evil of whatever Thou holdest by its forelock. O Allāh, Thou removest debt and sin. O Allāh, Thy army is never defeated, Thy promise never broken, and wealth avails its owner nothing before Thee. Glory be to Thee and praise.""**"
"17055","On the authority of al-Sāʾib ibn Mālik, who was with ʿUmārah when a man came and offered to teach words as if raised to the Prophet ﷺ: when you take your bed at night, say: O Allāh, I surrender myself to Thee, turn my face to Thee, entrust my affair to Thee, and lean my back upon Thee; I believe in Thy revealed Book and Thy sent Prophet; O Allāh, Thou created my soul, to Thee belong its life and death; if Thou take it, have mercy on it, and if Thou delay it, guard it with the guarding of faith. > **""O Allāh, I surrender myself to Thee, turn my face to Thee, entrust my affair to Thee, and lean my back upon Thee. I believe in Thy revealed Book and Thy sent Prophet. O Allāh, Thou created my soul; to Thee belong its life and its death. If Thou take it, have mercy on it, and if Thou delay it, guard it with the guarding of faith.""**"
"17056","On the authority of Ṣafiyyah and Duḥaybah, daughters of ʿUlaybah, that Qaylah bint Makhramah, may Allāh be pleased with her, when she took her share of bed after nightfall would say: in the Name of Allāh, I trust in Allāh, I lay my side for my Lord seeking His forgiveness for my sin, repeating it, then seeking refuge in Allāh and His perfect words which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from the evil of what descends and ascends, what enters the earth and issues from it, the trials of day and visitations of night except one bringing good, affirming faith and holding fast to Allāh, praising Him to Whose power, might, greatness and dominion all submit, then asking by the bonds of might of His Throne, the utmost of mercy in His Book, His exalted grandeur, His Greatest Name and His perfect words, for a merciful gaze forgiving every sin, mending poverty, destroying enemies, clothing the naked, settling debts, and granting all good of this world and the next, O Most Merciful, then glorifying Allāh thirty-three times, magnifying Him thirty-three, and praising Him thirty-four; then she would say: O my daughter, this is the seal's head, for the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came seeking a servant and he ﷺ said: shall I not guide you to better than a servant, and commanded her to this hundred at bed after nightfall. > **""Shall I not guide you to what is better for you than a servant? Say this hundred at your bed after nightfall.""**"
"17057","On the authority of Abū Juḥayfah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one of you rises from his sleep he should say: all praise belongs to Allāh Who restored our souls to us after we were as dead. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who restored our souls to us after we were as dead.""**"
"17058","Hind, the wife of Bilāl, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that Bilāl, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he took his bed would say: O Allāh, overlook my misdeeds and excuse me for my shortcomings. > **""O Allāh, overlook my misdeeds and excuse me for my shortcomings.""**"
"17059","Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he lay down would say: O Allāh, I ask Thee for sufficiency of family and servant, and I seek refuge in Thee lest a kin I severed supplicate against me. > **""O Allāh, I ask Thee for sufficiency of family and servant, and I seek refuge in Thee lest a kin I severed supplicate against me.""**"
"17060","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, whoever says when he stirs at night: in the Name of Allāh ten times, glory be to Allāh ten times, I believe in Allāh and disbelieve in jibt and ṭāghūt ten times, shall be guarded in all he fears, and no sin should overtake him until the like thereof. > **""In the Name of Allāh. Glory be to Allāh. I believe in Allāh and disbelieve in jibt and ṭāghūt.""**"
"17061","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no Muslim wakes in the night and says: there is no god but Allāh, Allāh is Greatest, glory be to Allāh, and all praise to Allāh, Lord of the worlds; O Allāh, forgive me, but he shall be forgiven, and if he resolves and rises and performs ablution and calls upon Allāh, He shall answer him. > **""There is no god but Allāh, Allāh is Greatest, glory be to Allāh, and all praise belongs to Allāh, Lord of the worlds. O Allāh, forgive me.""**"
"17062","On the authority of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever recites in a night: so whoever hopes for meeting his Lord, let him do righteous work and associate none in his Lord's worship, shall have light from ʿAdan Abyan to Makkah, filled with angels. > **""Whoever hopes for the meeting with his Lord, let him do righteous work and associate none in the worship of his Lord.""**"
"17063","On the authority of Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, who suffered wakefulness, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: shall I not teach you words which, when you say them, you shall sleep; say: O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade, Lord of the earths and what they bear, Lord of the devils and whom they mislead, be my Protector from the evil of all Thy creation, lest any of them transgress against me or oppress; mighty is Thy protection and blessed is Thy Name. > **""O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade, Lord of the earths and what they bear, Lord of the devils and whom they mislead, be my Protector from the evil of all Thy creation, lest any of them transgress against me or oppress. Mighty is Thy protection and blessed is Thy Name.""**"
"17064","A weak-chain variant of the preceding reports: be my Protector from all jinn and men, lest any of them transgress against me or harm me; mighty is Thy protection, exalted is Thy praise, and there is no god but Thee. > **""Be my Protector from all jinn and men, lest any of them transgress against me or harm me. Mighty is Thy protection, exalted is Thy praise, and there is no god but Thee.""**"
"17065","On the authority of Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, who would wake frightened at night and seize his sword striking whatever he met, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: shall I not teach you words taught me by the Trustworthy Spirit; he said: yes; he ﷺ said: say: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh, which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from the evil of what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the evil of night and day trials, and from every nighttime visitor except one bringing good, O Merciful; and he said them and it left him. > **""I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh, which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from the evil of what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the evil of the trials of night and day, and from every visitor except one that comes with good, O Merciful.""**"
"17066","On the authority of Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, who complained to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that he finds fright at night, he ﷺ said: shall I not teach you words taught me by Jibrīl, peace be upon him, who claimed a jinni ʿifrīt plotted against him; say: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh, which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from the evil of what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the evil of what He spread and created on earth and what issues from it, from the evil of night and day trials, and from the visitations of night and day except one bringing good, O Merciful. > **""I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh, which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from the evil of what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the evil of what He spread and created on earth and what issues from it, from the evil of the trials of night and day, and from the visitations of night and day except one that comes with good, O Merciful.""**"
"17067","On the authority of Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, told the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ of horrors he saw at night barring him from night prayer, and he ﷺ said: O Khālid ibn al-Walīd, shall I not teach you words, say them three times and Allāh shall remove that from you; he said: yes, O Messenger of Allāh, at the cost of my father and mother, for I complained only hoping this from you; he said: say: I seek refuge in the perfect word of Allāh from His anger and punishment, the evil of His servants, and the whisperings of devils and lest they attend me; ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, said: only a few nights passed until Khālid came and said: O Messenger of Allāh, by Him Who sent you with truth, I had not completed the words you taught me three times before Allāh removed what I felt, and I would not care if I entered a lion's den at night. > **""Say: I seek refuge in the perfect word of Allāh from His anger and punishment, the evil of His servants, and the whisperings of the devils and lest they attend me.""**"
"17068","Abū al-Tayyāḥ asked ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥunaysh al-Tamīmī, may Allāh be pleased with him, whether he met the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and how he ﷺ acted on the night the devils plotted against him, and he replied that the devils descended that night upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ from the valleys and ravines, among them a devil with a flame of fire in his hand seeking to burn the face of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so Jibrīl, peace be upon him, descended and said: O Muḥammad, say; he ﷺ asked what to say, and he told him to seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from the evil of what He created, originated and fashioned, from what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the trials of night and day, and from every visitor except one bringing good, O Merciful, whereupon their fire was extinguished and Allāh routed them. > **""I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from the evil of what He created, originated and fashioned, from the evil of what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the evil of the trials of night and day, and from the evil of every visitor except one that comes with good, O Merciful.""**"
"17069","In a variant of the preceding report, he was seized with dread, Jaʿfar said, I suppose he began to withdraw, and when he saw them he was alarmed, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to them."
"17070","On the authority of Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, who was with the Prophet ﷺ on the night the company of jinn were turned toward him, when a man of the jinn came with a flame of fire toward the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and Jibrīl said: O Muḥammad, shall I not teach you words which, when you say them, his flame shall be quenched and he shall fall upon his nostril; say: I seek refuge in the Noble Face of Allāh and His perfect words, which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from what He spread and created on earth and what issues from it, from the trials of night and day, and from the visitations of night and day except one bringing good, O Merciful. > **""I seek refuge in the Noble Face of Allāh and His perfect words, which none righteous or wicked can exceed, from the evil of what descends from heaven and ascends therein, from the evil of what He spread and created on earth and what issues from it, from the evil of the trials of night and day, and from the evil of the visitations of night and day except one that comes with good, O Merciful.""**"
"17071","On the authority of Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allāh be pleased with him, who suffered wakefulness at night and complained to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he ﷺ said: say: O Allāh, the stars have set, eyes have grown still, and Thou art Living, Sustaining; O Living, O Sustaining, put my eye to sleep and calm my night; and he said it and it left him. > **""O Allāh, the stars have set, eyes have grown still, and Thou art Living, Sustaining. O Living, O Sustaining, put my eye to sleep and calm my night.""**"
"17072","On the authority of al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allāh be pleased with him, a man complained to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ of loneliness, and he ﷺ said: say: glory be to the Sovereign, the Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit; Thou hast enveloped the heavens and the earth with might and omnipotence; and the man said it and Allāh removed his loneliness. > **""Glory be to the Sovereign, the Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit. Thou hast enveloped the heavens and the earth with might and omnipotence.""**"
"17073","On the authority of ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, a man came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ complaining of loneliness, and he ordered him to keep a pair of pigeons."
"17074","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: purify these bodies, may Allāh purify you, for no servant spends the night pure but an angel spends with him in his blanket, and whenever he turns in the night he says: O Allāh, forgive Thy servant, for he spent pure. > **""Purify these bodies, may Allāh purify you; for no servant spends the night pure but an angel spends with him in his blanket, and whenever he turns in the night he says: O Allāh, forgive Thy servant, for he spent pure.""**"
"17075","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever recites: say, He is Allāh, One, when entering his house, poverty shall be repelled from that house's people and the neighbours. > **""Whoever recites: Say, He is Allāh, One, when entering his house, poverty shall be repelled from that house's people and the neighbours.""**"
"17076","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he entered his house would recite Āyat al-Kursī in the corners of the house."
"17077","On the authority of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no Muslim leaves his house for travel or otherwise and says when he leaves: I believe in Allāh, I hold fast to Allāh, I trust in Allāh, there is no power nor strength but in Allāh, but he shall be granted the good of that exit and the evil of that exit shall be turned from him. > **""I believe in Allāh, I hold fast to Allāh, I trust in Allāh; there is no power nor strength but in Allāh.""**"
"17078","Zayd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khuṣayfah, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when he left his house would say: in the Name of Allāh, there is no power nor strength but in Allāh, what Allāh wills; I trust in Allāh, Allāh suffices me, and excellent Guardian. > **""In the Name of Allāh; there is no power nor strength but in Allāh; what Allāh wills. I trust in Allāh; Allāh suffices me, and excellent Guardian.""**"
"17079","ʿAwf reported that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he left his house would say: in the Name of Allāh, I trust in Allāh, there is no power nor strength but in Allāh; and Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī said: this is in the Qurʾān: embark therein, in the Name of Allāh, and he said: in Allāh we trust. > **""In the Name of Allāh, I trust in Allāh; there is no power nor strength but in Allāh.""**"
"17080","On the authority of Maymūnah, may Allāh be pleased with her, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ never left her house but raised his gaze to the sky and said: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee lest I stray or be led astray, slip or be made to slip, act ignorantly or be treated ignorantly, wrong or be wronged. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee lest I stray or be led astray, slip or be made to slip, act ignorantly or be treated ignorantly, wrong or be wronged.""**"
"17081","On the authority of Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ left the marketplace he would say: O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this market and the good of what is in it, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what is in it; O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee lest I incur therein a wicked oath or a losing bargain. > **""O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this market and the good of what is in it, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what is in it. O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee lest I incur therein a wicked oath or a losing bargain.""**"
"17082","Sulaym ibn Ḥanẓalah reported that ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, came to the market entrance and said: O Allāh, I ask Thee its good and the good of its people, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of its people. > **""O Allāh, I ask Thee its good and the good of its people, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of its people.""**"
"17083","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: O company of merchants, is one of you unable, when he returns from his market, to recite ten verses so Allāh writes for him a good deed for each verse. > **""O company of merchants, is one of you unable, when he returns from his market, to recite ten verses so Allāh writes for him a good deed for each verse?""**"
"17084","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ wished to depart on travel he would say: O Allāh, Thou art the Companion in travel and the Successor over family; O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from want in travel and sorrow upon return; O Allāh, contract the earth for us and ease travel for us; and when he wished to return he would say: returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord; and when he entered to his family he would say: repentance, repentance, returning to our Lord, leaving no sin upon us. > **""O Allāh, Thou art the Companion in travel and the Successor over family. O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from want in travel and sorrow upon return. O Allāh, contract the earth for us and ease travel for us.""**"
"17085","On the authority of al-Barāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ set out for travel he would say: O Allāh, a conveyance conveying good, forgiveness and pleasure from Thee; in Thy Hand is good, Thou hast power over all things; O Allāh, Thou art the Companion in travel and the Successor over family; O Allāh, ease travel for us and fold the earth for us; O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the hardship of travel and the sorrow of return. > **""O Allāh, a conveyance conveying good, forgiveness and pleasure from Thee. In Thy Hand is good; Thou hast power over all things. O Allāh, Thou art the Companion in travel and the Successor over family. O Allāh, ease travel for us and fold the earth for us. O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the hardship of travel and the sorrow of return.""**"
"17086","On the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Prophet ﷺ returned, and when he drew near Madinah he said: returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord; O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the hardship of travel, the sorrow of return, and an evil sight in family and wealth. > **""Returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord. O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the hardship of travel, the sorrow of return, and an evil sight in family and wealth.""**"
"17087","In a variant with him, he ﷺ would say it when he returned from an expedition."
"17088","On the authority of ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Prophet ﷺ wished to travel he would say: O Allāh, by Thee I assault, by Thee I range, and by Thee I journey. > **""O Allāh, by Thee I assault, by Thee I range, and by Thee I journey.""**"
"17089","On the authority of Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ travelled and came returning toward Madinah he would say: returning, praising our Lord, and worshipping our Lord. > **""Returning, praising our Lord, and worshipping our Lord.""**"
"17090","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one of you wishes to travel let him greet his brothers, for they shall increase him, through their supplication added to his, in good. > **""When one of you wishes to travel, let him greet his brothers, for they shall increase him, through their supplication added to his, in good.""**"
"17091","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ never intended travel but said when he rose from his sitting: O Allāh, by Thee I spread forth, to Thee I turn, by Thee I hold fast; O Allāh, Thou art my trust and my hope; O Allāh, suffice me what concerns me and what does not concern me and what Thou knowest better than I; provision me with taqwā, forgive my sin, and direct me to good wherever I turn; then he would go out. > **""O Allāh, by Thee I spread forth, to Thee I turn, and by Thee I hold fast. O Allāh, Thou art my trust and my hope. O Allāh, suffice me what concerns me and what does not concern me and what Thou knowest better than I; provision me with taqwā, forgive my sin, and direct me to good wherever I turn.""**"
"17092","Hishām ibn Qatādah al-Rahāwī, from his father Qatādah, may Allāh be pleased with him, said that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ appointed him over his people, he took his hand and bade farewell, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: may Allāh make taqwā your provision, forgive your sin, and direct you to good wherever you turn. > **""May Allāh make taqwā your provision, forgive your sin, and direct you to good wherever you turn.""**"
"17093","On the authority of Abū Lās al-Khuzāʿī, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ mounted them on charity camels, and they said they doubted the beasts would carry them, so he ﷺ said: there is no camel but upon its hump is a devil, so mention the Name of Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, when you ride as Allāh commanded you, then subdue them for yourselves, for they carry by Allāh's permission. > **""There is no camel but upon its hump is a devil, so mention the Name of Allāh when you ride as Allāh commanded you, then subdue them for yourselves, for they carry by Allāh's permission.""**"
"17094","Muḥammad ibn Ḥamzah ibn ʿAmr al-Aslamī heard his father say he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: upon every camel is a devil, so when you ride them pronounce Allāh's Name, Mighty and Majestic, and do not fall short of your needs. > **""Upon every camel is a devil, so when you ride them pronounce the Name of Allāh, and do not fall short of your needs.""**"
"17095","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that upon the top of every camel's hump is a devil, so subdue them. > **""Upon the top of every camel's hump is a devil, so subdue them.""**"
"17096","On the authority of ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no rider is alone on his journey with Allāh and His remembrance but an angel accompanies him, and none is alone with poetry and the like but a devil accompanies him. > **""No rider is alone on his journey with Allāh and His remembrance but an angel accompanies him, and none is alone with poetry and the like but a devil accompanies him.""**"
"17097","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said that when a man mounts his beast and does not mention Allāh's Name, Satan rides behind him and tells him to sing, and if he does not, tells him to wish."
"17098","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ seated him behind him on his mount, and when it stood level he ﷺ magnified Allāh thrice, praised Him thrice, glorified Him thrice, declared His unity once, then reclined and laughed, then turned to him and said: no man rides his mount and does as I did but Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, turns to him and laughs to him as I laughed to you. > **""No man rides his mount and does as I did but Allāh turns to him and laughs to him as I laughed to you.""**"
"17099","Abū Tamīmah al-Hujaymī, from one who rode behind the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, reported that he was his companion on a donkey, the donkey stumbled, and he said: wretched be Satan, so the Prophet ﷺ said: do not say wretched be Satan, for when you say it he magnifies himself and says: I felled him by my power, but when you say: in the Name of Allāh, he shrinks until he becomes smaller than a fly. > **""Do not say: wretched be Satan; but say: in the Name of Allāh.""**"
"17100","Abū al-Malīḥ ibn Usāmah, from his father, may Allāh be pleased with him, who was riding behind the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when their camel stumbled and he said: wretched be Satan, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: do not say wretched be Satan, for he grows until he becomes like a house and says: by my power, but say: in the Name of Allāh, for then he becomes like a fly. > **""Do not say: wretched be Satan, for he grows until he becomes like a house and says: by my power; but say: in the Name of Allāh, for then he becomes like a fly.""**"
"17101","On the authority of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that his nation's safety from drowning when they board the sea is to say: in the Name of Allāh be its course and anchorage; indeed my Lord is Forgiving, Merciful, and: they did not esteem Allāh as He deserves, the verse. > **""The safety of my nation from drowning when they board the sea is to say: In the Name of Allāh be its course and its anchorage; indeed my Lord is Forgiving, Merciful.""**"
"17102","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ, the safety of his nation from drowning when they board ships or the sea is to say: in the Name of Allāh, the King: and they did not esteem Allāh as He deserves, while the earth entirely shall be His grasp on Resurrection Day and the heavens folded in His Right; glory and exaltation to Him above what they associate, and: in the Name of Allāh be its course and anchorage; indeed my Lord is Forgiving, Merciful. > **""The safety of my nation from drowning when they board ships or the sea is to say: In the Name of Allāh, the King. And: In the Name of Allāh be its course and its anchorage; indeed my Lord is Forgiving, Merciful.""**"
"17103","On the authority of ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān, may Allāh be pleased with him, from the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ, when one of you loses something or seeks help in a land without company, let him say: O servants of Allāh, aid me, for Allāh has servants we do not see; and it has been tried. > **""When one of you loses something or seeks help in a land without company, let him say: O servants of Allāh, aid me; for Allāh has servants we do not see.""**"
"17104","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh has angels on earth besides the guardians who record what falls of tree leaves, so when one of you is lamed in a barren land let him call: aid, O servants of Allāh. > **""Allāh has angels on earth besides the guardians who record what falls of tree leaves, so when one of you is stricken in a barren land let him call: aid, O servants of Allāh.""**"
"17105","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one's beast escapes in a barren land let him call: O servants of Allāh, restrain it, O servants of Allāh, restrain it, for Allāh has one present on earth who shall restrain it for you. > **""When one's beast escapes in a barren land, let him call: O servants of Allāh, restrain it, O servants of Allāh, restrain it; for Allāh has one present on earth who shall restrain it for you.""**"
"17106","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the lost item, that he says: O Allāh, Restorer of the lost, Guide of the astray, Who guides from error, return my lost thing to me by Thy power and authority, for it is of Thy gift and bounty. > **""O Allāh, Restorer of the lost, Guide of the astray, Who guides from error, return my lost thing to me by Thy power and authority, for it is of Thy gift and bounty.""**"
"17107","On the authority of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever alights at a lodging and says: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from the evil of what He created, shall see nothing therein he dislikes until he departs; his father said: I met ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis in a dream and asked: did the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ narrate this to you, and he said: yes. > **""Whoever alights at a lodging and says: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from the evil of what He created, shall see nothing therein he dislikes until he departs.""**"
"17108","On the authority of Khawlah bint Ḥakīm, may Allāh be pleased with her, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever alights at a lodging and says: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from the evil of what He created, nothing therein shall harm him until he moves from it. > **""Whoever alights at a lodging and says: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allāh from the evil of what He created, nothing therein shall harm him until he moves from it.""**"
"17109","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr, may Allāh be pleased with him, who left Ḥimṣ and night overtook him at al-Buqayʿah, and the dwellers of the earth came upon him, so he recited this verse of Sūrat al-Aʿrāf: indeed your Lord is Allāh Who created the heavens and the earth, to the end of the verse, whereupon some said to others: silence him now until morning, and when morning came he mounted his beast."
"17110","Qatādah said he heard Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, say: when we alighted at a lodging we would glorify until the saddles were untied; Shuʿbah said: glorifying with the tongue."
"17111","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ ascended an elevated part of earth he would say: O Allāh, to Thee belongs loftiness over every loftiness, and praise in every state. > **""O Allāh, to Thee belongs loftiness over every loftiness, and praise in every state.""**"
"17112","On the authority of Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: O Jubayr, would you love, when you go on travel, to be the finest of your companions in appearance and the most abundant in provision; he said: yes, at the cost of my father and mother; he said: then recite these five sūrahs: Say, O disbelievers, When Allāh's help and victory come, Say, He is Allāh, One, Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, and Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind, opening each sūrah with: in the Name of Allāh, Most Gracious, Most Merciful, and sealing your recitation with it; Jubayr said: I was wealthy with much money, yet when I went with whomsoever Allāh willed on travel I was the shabbiest in appearance and scantest in provision, but since the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ taught them to me and I recited them, I became the finest in appearance and most abundant in provision until I returned from that travel. > **""Would you love, when you go on travel, to be the finest of your companions in appearance and the most abundant in provision? Then recite these five sūrahs, opening each with: In the Name of Allāh, Most Gracious, Most Merciful, and sealing your recitation with it.""**"
"17113","On the authority of Saʿd, meaning Ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ commanded us, when the ghoul appears to us or when we see the ghoul, to call the adhān. > **""When the ghoul appears to you, or when you see the ghoul, call the adhān.""**"
"17114","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when the ghoul appears to you, call the adhān, for when Satan hears the call he turns away swiftly. > **""When the ghoul appears to you, call the adhān; for when Satan hears the call he turns away swiftly.""**"
"17115","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, they would travel with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and when he saw a town he wished to enter he would say thrice: O Allāh, bless us therein, then: O Allāh, grant us its produce, endear us to its people, and endear its righteous people to us. > **""O Allāh, bless us therein. O Allāh, grant us its produce, endear us to its people, and endear its righteous people to us.""**"
"17116","On the authority of Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ wished to enter a town he would not enter until he said: O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shaded, Lord of the seven earths and what they bore, Lord of the winds and what they scattered, Lord of the devils and whom they misled, I ask Thee its good and the good of what is in it, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what is in it. > **""O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shaded, Lord of the seven earths and what they bore, Lord of the winds and what they scattered, Lord of the devils and whom they misled, I ask Thee its good and the good of what is in it, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what is in it.""**"
"17117","On the authority of Abū Mughīth ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ overlooked Khaybar he told his Companions to halt, then said: O Allāh, Lord of the heavens and what they shaded, Lord of the earths and what they bore, Lord of the devils and whom they misled, Lord of the winds and what they scattered, I ask Thee the good of this town and the good of its people, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil, the evil of its people, and the evil of what is in it; advance in the Name of Allāh; and he would say it for every town he wished to enter. > **""O Allāh, Lord of the heavens and what they shaded, Lord of the earths and what they bore, Lord of the devils and whom they misled, Lord of the winds and what they scattered, I ask Thee the good of this town and the good of its people, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil, the evil of its people, and the evil of what is in it. Advance in the Name of Allāh.""**"
"17118","ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Marwān, from his father, reported that Kaʿb swore to him by Him Who parted the sea for Mūsā that Ṣuhayb, may Allāh be pleased with him, narrated to him that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ never saw a town he wished to enter but said when he saw it: O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shaded, and Lord of the seven earths and what they bore, and Lord of the devils and whom they misled, and Lord of the winds and what they scattered, we ask Thee the good of this town, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil, the evil of its people, and the evil of what is in it. > **""O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shaded, and Lord of the seven earths and what they bore, and Lord of the devils and whom they misled, and Lord of the winds and what they scattered, we ask Thee the good of this town, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil, the evil of its people, and the evil of what is in it.""**"
"17119","Qatādah reported that Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he wished to enter a town would say: O Allāh, Lord of the heavens and what they shaded, Lord of the devils and whom they misled, and Lord of the winds and what they scattered, I ask Thee its good and the good of what is in it, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what is in it. > **""O Allāh, Lord of the heavens and what they shaded, Lord of the devils and whom they misled, and Lord of the winds and what they scattered, I ask Thee its good and the good of what is in it, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what is in it.""**"
"17120","Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl asked Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, what the people feared when they entered or overlooked a town that they would say: O Allāh, grant us sustenance therein, and he replied that they feared the injustice of rulers and the withholding of rain. > **""O Allāh, grant us sustenance therein.""**"
"17121","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, when a strong wind raged the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of what it was commanded, and seek refuge in Thee from the evil of what it was commanded. > **""O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of what it was commanded, and seek refuge in Thee from the evil of what it was commanded.""**"
"17122","In a variant, when he ﷺ saw the wind he was alarmed."
"17123","On the authority of ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the north wind blew fiercely the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the evil of what was sent therein. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the evil of what was sent therein.""**"
"17124","On the authority of Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the wind blew fiercely the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, make it fertile, not barren. > **""O Allāh, make it fertile, not barren.""**"
"17125","On the authority of ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the north wind blew fiercely the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the evil of what it was sent with. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the evil of what it was sent with.""**"
"17126","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, when a wind raged the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would face it, kneel upon his knees, extend his hands, and say: O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this wind and the good of what it was sent with, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what it was sent with; O Allāh, make it mercy and not punishment; O Allāh, make it winds of mercy and not a wind of torment. > **""O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this wind and the good of what it was sent with, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil and the evil of what it was sent with. O Allāh, make it mercy and not punishment. O Allāh, make it winds of mercy and not a wind of torment.""**"
"17127","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when you hear the sound of thunder, remember Allāh, for it does not strike one who remembers. > **""When you hear the sound of thunder, remember Allāh, for it does not strike one who remembers.""**"
"17128","On the authority of Abū Saʿīd, meaning al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, on the Day of the Trench they said: O Messenger of Allāh, is there anything we may say when hearts have reached throats, and he ﷺ said: yes; O Allāh, cover our faults and calm our fears; whereupon Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, struck the faces of their enemies with wind, and Allāh routed them with wind. > **""O Allāh, cover our faults and calm our fears.""**"
"17129","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no one is ever struck by worry or grief and says: O Allāh, I am Thy servant, son of Thy servant, son of Thy maid; my forelock is in Thy Hand; Thy decree over me proceeds; Thy judgment over me is just; I ask Thee by every Name of Thine, by which Thou named Thyself, or sent down in Thy Book, or taught to any of Thy creation, or kept with Thee in the knowledge of the unseen, to make the Qurʾān the spring of my heart, the light of my breast, the clearing of my sorrow, and the departure of my worry, but Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, shall remove his worry and replace his grief with joy; they said: O Messenger of Allāh, should we learn these words, and he ﷺ said: yes, whoever hears them should learn them. > **""O Allāh, I am Thy servant, son of Thy servant, son of Thy maid. My forelock is in Thy Hand; Thy decree over me proceeds; Thy judgment over me is just. I ask Thee by every Name of Thine, by which Thou named Thyself, or sent down in Thy Book, or taught to any of Thy creation, or kept with Thee in the knowledge of the unseen, to make the Qurʾān the spring of my heart, the light of my breast, the clearing of my sorrow, and the departure of my worry.""**"
"17130","On the authority of Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, whoever is struck by worry or grief should supplicate with these words: O Allāh, I am Thy servant, son of Thy servant, and son of Thy maid; my forelock is in Thy Hand; Thy decree over me proceeds; Thy judgment over me is just; I ask Thee by every Name of Thine, by which Thou named Thyself, or sent down in Thy Book, or taught to any of Thy creation, or kept with Thee in the knowledge of the unseen, to make the Qurʾān the spring of my heart, the light of my breast, the clearing of my sorrow, and the departure of my worry; one said: O Messenger of Allāh, indeed deprived is he who is deprived of these words, and he ﷺ said: yes; say them and teach them, for whoever says them and teaches them seeking what is in them, Allāh shall remove his distress and prolong his joy. > **""O Allāh, I am Thy servant, son of Thy servant, and son of Thy maid. My forelock is in Thy Hand; Thy decree over me proceeds; Thy judgment over me is just. I ask Thee by every Name of Thine, by which Thou named Thyself, or sent down in Thy Book, or taught to any of Thy creation, or kept with Thee in the knowledge of the unseen, to make the Qurʾān the spring of my heart, the light of my breast, the clearing of my sorrow, and the departure of my worry.""**"
"17131","On the authority of Abū Bakrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the words of the distressed are: O Allāh, I hope for Thy mercy, so entrust me not to myself for a blink of an eye, and set right all my affair. > **""O Allāh, I hope for Thy mercy, so entrust me not to myself for a blink of an eye, and set right all my affair.""**"
"17132","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ grasped the two doorposts while they were in the house and said: O sons of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, when distress, hardship, or affliction befalls you, say: Allāh, Allāh, our Lord, we associate nothing with Him. > **""Allāh, Allāh, our Lord, we associate nothing with Him.""**"
"17133","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to a group of Banū Hāshim: is there anyone not of you with you; they said: no, except our nephew or our servant; and he ﷺ said: when one of you is struck by worry or hardship, let him say: Allāh, Allāh, my Lord, I associate nothing with Him. > **""Allāh, Allāh, my Lord, I associate nothing with Him.""**"
"17134","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever says: there is no god but Allāh before all things, there is no god but Allāh after all things, there is no god but Allāh Who remains while all things perish, shall be granted health from worry and grief. > **""There is no god but Allāh before all things; there is no god but Allāh after all things; there is no god but Allāh Who remains while all things perish.""**"
"17135","On the authority of Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, when one of you fears the ruler let him say: O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and Lord of the Mighty Throne, be my Protector from the evil of so-and-so, son of so-and-so, meaning whom he intends, and from the evil of jinn and men and their followers, lest any of them transgress against me; mighty is Thy protection, exalted is Thy praise, and there is no god but Thee. > **""O Allāh, Lord of the seven heavens and Lord of the Mighty Throne, be my Protector from the evil of so-and-so, son of so-and-so, and from the evil of jinn and men and their followers, lest any of them transgress against me. Mighty is Thy protection, exalted is Thy praise, and there is no god but Thee.""**"
"17136","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, said that when you come to an awesome ruler fearing he may assault you, say thrice: Allāh is Greatest, Allāh is Greater than all His creation, Allāh is Mightier than what I fear and dread, and I seek refuge in Allāh, besides Whom there is no god, Who holds the seven heavens from falling upon earth except by His leave, from the evil of Thy servant so-and-so, his armies, followers, and partisans of jinn and men; my God, be my Protector from their evil; exalted is Thy praise, mighty is Thy protection, blessed is Thy Name, and there is no god but Thee. > **""Allāh is Greatest; Allāh is Greater than all His creation; Allāh is Mightier than what I fear and dread. I seek refuge in Allāh, besides Whom there is no god, Who holds the seven heavens from falling upon earth except by His leave, from the evil of Thy servant so-and-so, his armies, followers, and partisans of jinn and men. My God, be my Protector from their evil. Exalted is Thy praise, mighty is Thy protection, blessed is Thy Name, and there is no god but Thee.""**"
"17137","On the authority of Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when a grave calamity befalls or a dark wind rages, hold to takbīr, for it dispels the black dust. > **""When a grave calamity befalls or a dark wind rages, hold to takbīr, for it dispels the black dust.""**"
"17138","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one of you sees another in affliction let him say: all praise belongs to Allāh Who granted me health from what He afflicted you with, and favoured me greatly over many of His creation; for when he says that he shall be grateful for that blessing. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who granted me health from what He afflicted you with, and favoured me greatly over many of His creation.""**"
"17139","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever sees an afflicted one and says: all praise belongs to Allāh Who granted me health from what He afflicted you with, and favoured me greatly over many of His creation, that affliction shall not strike him. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who granted me health from what He afflicted you with, and favoured me greatly over many of His creation.""**"
"17140","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, we were forbidden to follow the stars with our eyes when they fall, and commanded to say then: what Allāh wills; there is no power but in Allāh. > **""What Allāh wills; there is no power but in Allāh.""**"
"17141","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: extinguish the blaze with takbīr. > **""Extinguish the blaze with takbīr.""**"
"17142","On the authority of Abū Rāfiʿ, servant of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one's ear rings let him remember me and send blessings upon me and say: may Allāh mention with good whoever mentioned me with it. > **""When one's ear rings, let him remember me and send blessings upon me and say: may Allāh mention with good whoever mentioned me with it.""**"
"17143","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ looked in the mirror he would say: all praise belongs to Allāh Who fashioned my creation, made my form beautiful, and adorned me where He disfigured others. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who fashioned my creation, made my form beautiful, and adorned me where He disfigured others.""**"
"17144","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ looked in the mirror he would say: all praise belongs to Allāh Who made my character and creation beautiful, and adorned me where He disfigured others; and when he applied kohl he would put two in each eye and one between them, and when he dressed he began with the right, and when he undressed he removed the left, and when he entered the mosque he entered his right foot, and he loved the right in all things, in taking and giving. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who made my character and creation beautiful, and adorned me where He disfigured others.""**"
"17145","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ looked at his face in the mirror he would say: all praise belongs to Allāh Who fashioned my creation and proportioned it, shaped the form of my creation and made it beautiful, and made me among the Muslims. > **""All praise belongs to Allāh Who fashioned my creation and proportioned it, shaped the form of my creation and made it beautiful, and made me among the Muslims.""**"
"17146","On the authority of ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw the crescent he would say: Allāh is Greatest; all praise belongs to Allāh; there is no power nor strength but in Allāh; O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this month, and seek refuge in Thee from the evil of decree and the evil of the gathering. > **""Allāh is Greatest; all praise belongs to Allāh; there is no power nor strength but in Allāh. O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this month, and seek refuge in Thee from the evil of decree and the evil of the gathering.""**"
"17147","On the authority of Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, may Allāh be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw the crescent he would say: a crescent of good and guidance; then he would say thrice: O Allāh, I ask Thee its good; O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this month and the good of decree, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil, thrice. > **""A crescent of good and guidance. O Allāh, I ask Thee the good of this month and the good of decree, and seek refuge in Thee from its evil.""**"
"17148","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw the crescent he would say: O Allāh, raise it over us with security and faith, safety and submission, and success in what Thou lovest and pleasest; our Lord and your Lord is Allāh. > **""O Allāh, raise it over us with security and faith, safety and submission, and success in what Thou lovest and pleasest. Our Lord and your Lord is Allāh.""**"
"17149","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, when he saw the crescent he would say: a crescent of good and guidance; I believe in Him Who created you and proportioned you. > **""A crescent of good and guidance. I believe in Him Who created you and proportioned you.""**"
"17150","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hishām reported that the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, may Allāh be pleased with them, would learn this supplication when the year or month began: O Allāh, bring it upon us with security and faith, safety and submission, pleasure from the Merciful, and protection from Satan. > **""O Allāh, bring it upon us with security and faith, safety and submission, pleasure from the Merciful, and protection from Satan.""**"
"17151","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no servant receives a blessing in family, wealth, or child and says: what Allāh wills; there is no power but in Allāh, but he shall see therein no affliction short of death; and he recited: and why, when you entered your garden, did you not say: what Allāh wills; there is no power but in Allāh. > **""No servant receives a blessing in family, wealth, or child and says: what Allāh wills; there is no power but in Allāh, but he shall see therein no affliction short of death.""**"
"17152","On the authority of ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever is blessed and desires its continuance should often say: there is no power nor strength but in Allāh; then the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ recited: and why, when you entered your garden, did you not say: what Allāh wills; there is no power but in Allāh. > **""Whoever is blessed and desires its continuance should often say: there is no power nor strength but in Allāh.""**"
"17153","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever is clothed by Allāh with a blessing should often praise Allāh, whoever has many sins should seek Allāh's forgiveness, and whoever's sustenance is delayed should often say: there is no power nor strength but in Allāh. > **""Whoever is clothed by Allāh with a blessing should often praise Allāh; whoever has many sins should seek Allāh's forgiveness; and whoever's sustenance is delayed should often say: there is no power nor strength but in Allāh.""**"
"17154","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to a man: how are you this morning, O so-and-so; he said: I praise Allāh to you, O Messenger of Allāh; and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: that is what I wanted from you. > **""I praise Allāh to you.""**"
"17155","Yūnus ibn Maysarah ibn Ḥalbas said he met Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, greeted him, and asked: how are you, O Abū Shaddād, may Allāh set you right; and he replied: well, O son of my brother."
"17156","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he passed a people standing upon their beasts and mounts and said to them: ride them sound and leave them sound, and do not take them as chairs for your talk in roads and markets, for often a mount is better than its rider and more remembering of Allāh, Blessed and Exalted, than he. > **""Ride them sound and leave them sound, and do not take them as chairs for your talk in roads and markets; for often a mount is better than its rider and more remembering of Allāh than he.""**"
"17157","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever hears the sound of a bell, or enters a synagogue, church, fire-house, or idol-house, and says: there is no god but Allāh, and we worship none but Him, there shall be written for him reward equal to those who did not say it, or he shall be written with Allāh as a truthful one. > **""There is no god but Allāh, and we worship none but Him.""**"
"17158","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one of you buys a servant let him grasp her forelock and say: O Allāh, I ask Thee her good and the good of what Thou formed her upon; and when he buys a camel let him grasp the top of its hump and say the like. > **""O Allāh, I ask Thee her good and the good of what Thou formed her upon.""**"
"17159","Yazīd ibn al-Hād, from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, reported that it reached him that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: no man sits in a gathering then says when he wishes to rise: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; there is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee, but he shall be forgiven what was in that gathering; and he narrated it to Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah, who said: thus al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, narrated to me from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. There is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17160","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the expiation of the gathering is to say: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17161","On the authority of Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would not rise from a gathering until he said: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee; then he would say: it is expiation for what occurs in the gathering. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17162","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say that the expiation of the gathering is for the servant to say after he rises: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; there is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. There is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17163","On the authority of al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allāh be pleased with him, they said: O Messenger of Allāh, when we rise from you we take to the talk of Jāhiliyyah, and he ﷺ said: when you sit in such gatherings you fear for yourselves, say when you rise: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; we testify there is no god but Thou; we seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee; it shall expiate what you incurred therein. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. We testify there is no god but Thou; we seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17164","On the authority of Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, may Allāh be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, the expiation of the gathering is not to rise until one says thrice: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; there is no god but Thou; turn to me and forgive me; if it was an idle gathering it shall be its expiation, and if it was a gathering of remembrance it shall be a seal upon it. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. There is no god but Thou; turn to me and forgive me.""**"
"17165","On the authority of Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever says: glory be to Allāh and praise; glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; I testify there is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee, and says it in a gathering of remembrance, it shall be a seal stamped upon it, and whoever says it in an idle gathering, it shall be expiation for him. > **""Glory be to Allāh and praise. Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. I testify there is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17166","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ, the expiation of the gathering is: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; I testify there is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. I testify there is no god but Thou; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17167","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ raised his head toward the ceiling of the house he would say: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee; ʿĀʾishah asked him about them and he said: I was commanded with them. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17168","On the authority of Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, before he died the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would often say: glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise; I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee; and he said: I was commanded, then recited: when Allāh's help and victory come. > **""Glory be to Thee, O Allāh, and praise. I seek Thy forgiveness and repent to Thee.""**"
"17169","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever seeks refuge in Allāh ten times daily from Satan, Allāh shall appoint for him an angel to repel the devils from him. > **""Whoever seeks refuge in Allāh ten times daily from Satan, Allāh shall appoint for him an angel to repel the devils from him.""**"
"17170","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mawhab reported that ʿUthmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, told Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, to go as judge, and he asked: will you excuse me, O Commander of the Faithful; he insisted, and Ibn ʿUmar said: do not hasten, I heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: whoever seeks refuge in Allāh has taken a strong refuge; he said: yes; so Ibn ʿUmar said: I seek refuge in Allāh from being a judge; and ʿUthmān asked what prevents him when his father judged, and he replied that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: whoever judges among people unjustly enters the Fire, whoever judges ignorantly is of the Fire's people, and whoever judges knowingly with truth or justice shall wish to return even. > **""Whoever seeks refuge in Allāh has taken a strong refuge.""**"
"17171","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from knowledge that does not benefit, a heart that does not humble itself, a soul never sated, and a supplication not heard; O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from these four. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from knowledge that does not benefit, a heart that does not humble itself, a soul never sated, and a supplication not heard. O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from these four.""**"
"17172","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from inability and laziness, and seek refuge in Thee from hardness, heedlessness, poverty, humility, and destitution, and seek refuge in Thee from immorality, discord, hypocrisy, repute, and showing off, and seek refuge in Thee from deafness, dumbness, madness, leprosy, and evil diseases. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from inability and laziness, and seek refuge in Thee from hardness, heedlessness, poverty, humility, and destitution, and seek refuge in Thee from immorality, discord, hypocrisy, repute, and showing off, and seek refuge in Thee from deafness, dumbness, madness, leprosy, and evil diseases.""**"
"17173","On the authority of Jarīr, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ would supplicate: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from a supplication not heard, a heart not humbled, and a soul never sated. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from a supplication not heard, a heart not humbled, and a soul never sated.""**"
"17174","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Prophet ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the overwhelming of debt, the overwhelming of the enemy, the ruin of the widowed, and the trial of the Dajjāl. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the overwhelming of debt, the overwhelming of the enemy, the ruin of the widowed, and the trial of the Dajjāl.""**"
"17175","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from laziness, extreme age, the trial of the breast, and the punishment of the grave. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from laziness, extreme age, the trial of the breast, and the punishment of the grave.""**"
"17176","On the authority of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, may Allāh be pleased with them, he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: I seek refuge in Thy Noble Face and Thy Noble Name from disbelief and poverty. > **""I seek refuge in Thy Noble Face and Thy Noble Name from disbelief and poverty.""**"
"17177","On the authority of ʿUbādah, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: seek refuge in Allāh from poverty and need, and from wronging or being wronged. > **""Seek refuge in Allāh from poverty and need, and from wronging or being wronged.""**"
"17178","ʿAwn ibn ʿAbd Allāh reported that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, would seek refuge from four, saying: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from wealth that makes me transgress, poverty that makes me forget, desire that destroys me, and a deed that disgraces me. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from wealth that makes me transgress, poverty that makes me forget, desire that destroys me, and a deed that disgraces me.""**"
"17179","On the authority of ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from an evil day, an evil night, an evil hour, an evil companion, and an evil neighbour in the abode of residence. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from an evil day, an evil night, an evil hour, an evil companion, and an evil neighbour in the abode of residence.""**"
"17180","Jubayr ibn Nufayr reported that ʿAwf ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, went out to the people and said: the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ commanded you to seek refuge from three: greed where there is no place for greed, greed returning to nature, and greed toward no object. > **""Seek refuge from greed where there is no place for greed, greed returning to nature, and greed toward no object.""**"
"17181","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Prophet ﷺ said: seek refuge in Allāh from greed leading to greed, from greed toward no object, and from greed where there is no place for greed. > **""Seek refuge in Allāh from greed leading to greed, from greed toward no object, and from greed where there is no place for greed.""**"
"17182","On the authority of al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib al-Kindī, may Allāh be pleased with him, he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: seek refuge in Allāh from greed leading to greed, and from greed leading to no object. > **""Seek refuge in Allāh from greed leading to greed, and from greed leading to no object.""**"
"17183","ʿĀʾishah bint Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the evil of the two blind ones; it was said: O Messenger of Allāh, what are the two blind ones; and he ﷺ said: the flood and the aggressive camel. > **""O Allāh, I seek refuge in Thee from the evil of the two blind ones: the flood and the aggressive camel.""**"
"17184","On the authority of ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: O people, go out little after the stillness of night, for Allāh, Exalted, spreads creatures upon the earth doing as commanded; and when you hear the braying of donkeys or the barking of dogs, seek refuge in Allāh from Satan, for they see what you do not see. > **""Go out little after the stillness of night; and when you hear the braying of donkeys or the barking of dogs, seek refuge in Allāh from Satan, for they see what you do not see.""**"
"17185","On the authority of Ṣuhayb, may Allāh be pleased with him, he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: when the donkey brays, seek refuge in Allāh from the accursed Satan. > **""When the donkey brays, seek refuge in Allāh from the accursed Satan.""**"
"17186","On the authority of Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever intends a good deed and does not do it, one good is written for him, and if he does it, ten like it up to seven hundred and sevenfold are written for him; and whoever intends an evil deed and does not do it, nothing is written against him, and if he does it, one evil is written against him, or Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, effaces it. > **""Whoever intends a good deed and does not do it, one good is written for him, and if he does it, ten like it up to seven hundred and sevenfold are written for him. Whoever intends an evil deed and does not do it, nothing is written against him, and if he does it, one evil is written against him.""**"
"17187","On the authority of Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever intends a good deed and does not do it, one good is written for him, and if he does it, ten are written for him; and whoever intends an evil deed and does not do it, nothing is written against him, and if he does it, one evil is written. > **""Whoever intends a good deed and does not do it, one good is written for him, and if he does it, ten are written for him. Whoever intends an evil deed and does not do it, nothing is written against him, and if he does it, one evil is written.""**"
"17188","Abū ʿUthmān, meaning al-Nahdī, said it reached him from Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, that Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, gives His servant a million good deeds for one good deed; then he went for ḥajj or ʿumrah and met him and said: it reached me from you that you heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, gives His believing servant a million good deeds for one good; and Abū Hurayrah said: no; rather I heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, gives him two million good deeds; then he recited: He shall multiply it and grant from Himself an immense reward; and said: when Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, says: an immense reward, who can estimate it. > **""Allāh gives him two million good deeds for one good.""**"
"17189","In a variant, he came to Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, and said: it reached me that you say one good is multiplied a millionfold; and he said: why do you wonder at that; by Allāh, I heard him, and he mentioned the like. > **""Why do you wonder at that? By Allāh, I heard him.""**"
"17190","On the authority of ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, said to ʿAlī: O son of my uncle, work and the millstone burden me, so speak to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ; he said: yes; and the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ came to them next morning while they slept in one blanket, and put his feet between them, and Fāṭimah said: O Prophet of Allāh, work burdens me, so if you order me a servant from what Allāh granted you as fayʾ; and he ﷺ said: shall I not teach you what is better for you than that; glorify thirty-three, praise thirty-three, and magnify thirty-four, making a hundred upon the tongue and a thousand upon the scale, for Allāh says: whoever brings a good deed shall have ten like it, up to a hundred thousand. > **""Shall I not teach you what is better for you than that? Glorify thirty-three, praise thirty-three, and magnify thirty-four, making a hundred upon the tongue and a thousand upon the scale.""**"
